than the criss-cross algorithm as apparent from the results at the bottom of Table 
\ref{tab:epsvarious_alg1_vs_alg3}. In these examples, the criss-cross algorithm
is not capable of returning highly accurate results probably because the eigenvalue
problems it solves are ill-conditioned.
We observe in general that for larger values of $\epsilon$ close to $\sigma_{\min}(M)$,
Algorithm \ref{alg:fixed_point2m} still converges to $\alpha_{\epsilon}(P(\cdot;\nu))$
robustly even if it is at the expense of additional iterations, while Algorithm \ref{alg:fixed_point}
may possibly not converge.


