\section{MLP Probe Details}
\label{app:mlp_probe_results}

As explained in section~\ref{sec:rep_analysis}, we extracted representations from Llama-3-8B-Instruct and Llama-3-8B-Instruct-RR to construct datasets for training MLP classifiers. For each model, we constructed a dataset $\mathcal{D}_{rep}=\{(\mathbf{x}_i,y_i)\}_{i=1}^{N}$, where $\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{R}^{4096}$ is a response token representation vector with label $y=1$ if it belongs to the circuit breaker dataset, and 0 if it belongs to the retain dataset. We then performed a random 80/20 split to obtain $\mathcal{D}_{train}$ and $\mathcal{D}_{test}$ and trained the MLP classifiers using the following scikit-learn specification:

\texttt{MLPClassifier(hidden\_layer\_sizes=(64,32), max\_iter=1000, random\_state=42)}

The MLP classifiers trained on the original Llama model and circuit breaker model representations achieved \textbf{0.997} and \textbf{0.999} test accuracies, respectively, indicating that they can distinguish between benign and harmful single-turn examples with very high accuracy.


