\subsubsection{Detection Head}
%检测头使用锚定框来检测不同尺寸和纵横比的目标。这些锚定框用于确定检测目标的边界框，可以显著减少搜索空间，提高检测效率。每个锚定框都有一个特定的大小和纵横比。每种尺度的输出特征图都会生成三种尺度的锚框的输出特征图，三种尺度的特征图共生成9种尺度的锚框。使用长宽为[(10,13), (16,30), (33,23)]的三个大小的预设锚框处理8倍下采样倍数的输出特征图，同样地，[(30,61), (62,45), (59,119)]处理16倍下采样倍数的输出特征图以及[(116,90), (156,198), (373,326)]大小锚框处理32倍下采样倍数的输出特征图.
The detection head employs preset anchor boxes to identify targets of varying scales. The aspect ratios of these anchor boxes are determined based on statistical analysis of the labeled data. Quick Response (QR) codes are square with an aspect ratio of 1:1, while houses and nests, though their shapes vary depending on the viewing angle, generally exhibit aspect ratios close to 1:2 or 2:1. As a result, the anchor box aspect ratios are set to 1:1 (square), 2:1 (wide rectangle), and 1:2 (tall rectangle), allowing for the detection of objects with different shapes. These anchor boxes help substantially reduce the search space and improve detection efficiency.

To further improve detection across scales, each output feature map generates three sets of anchor boxes corresponding to different object sizes, resulting in a total of nine anchor boxes. For small objects, the feature maps with 8$\times$ downsampling utilize small anchor boxes with sizes \{(10,13), (16,30), (33,23)\}. For 16$\times$ downsampled feature maps, medium anchor boxes with sizes \{(30,61), (62,45), (59,119)\} are used. Similarly, for 32$\times$ downsampled feature maps, large anchor boxes with sizes \{(116,90), (156,198), (373,326)\} are employed to detect large objects.


%检测头的损失
We set the detection loss as the weighted sum of the classification loss, object loss, and bounding box loss.

{\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{det}} = \frac{3}{N_{l}}*\mathcal{L}_{\text{class}} +\frac{N_{c}}{N_{l}} *\mathcal{L}_{\text{obj}}+\left(\frac{N_{\text{imgsz}}}{N_{\text{model}}}\right)^2 * \frac{3}{N_{l}}* \mathcal{L}_{\text{box}},
\label{eq:detloss}
\end{equation}}
where $N_{l}$ is number of detection layer, $N_{c}$ is number of target detection categories, $N_{\text{imgsz}}$ is the image size, and $N_{\text{model}}$ is the dimension of the model input.
$\mathcal{L}_{\text{obj}}$ and $\mathcal{L}_{\text{class}}$ are focal loss~\cite{lin2017focal}

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{Focal}}(p_t) = -\alpha_t (1 - p_t)^\gamma \log(p_t),
\label{eq2}
\end{equation}
where, $p_t$ is the predicted probability, $\alpha_t$ is the class weight, and $\gamma$ is the tuning parameter. 

We employ focal loss to address the issue of class imbalance. When a sample is difficult to classify, $p_t$ approaches 1, resulting in a smaller value of $(1 - p_t)^\gamma$, thereby reducing the loss weight for difficult samples. On the other hand, when a sample is easy to classify, $p_t$ is close to 0, resulting in a higher value of $(1 - p_t)^\gamma$, which increases the loss weight for easy samples. This encourages the model to focus on difficult samples.

In Equation~\eqref{eq:detloss}, $\mathcal{L}_{\text{box}}$ is the Complete Intersection over Union~\cite{zheng2021enhancing} (CIoU) loss:

\begin{equation}
\text{CIoU} = \text{IoU} - \frac{{\rho^2(c_2, c_1)}}{{\rho^2(c_2, c_1) + \alpha}} + \text{v}(b_1, b_2),
\label{eq3}
\end{equation}
where $\rho^2(c_2, c_1)$ is the square of the distance between the center points of two boxes $c_2$ and $c_1$, $\alpha$ is a smoothing parameter to prevent zero division when the centers of two boxes coincide, and $\text{v}(b_1, b_2)$ penalizes the deviation between two bounding boxes $b_1$ and $b_2$, by the differences in center distance, width, and height. The penalty term $\text{v}(b_1, b_2)$ is 
%该惩罚项考虑了两个边界框之间的中心距离、宽度和高度的差异。其公式如下：$\text{IoU}$ is the Intersection over Union,
\begin{equation}
\text{v}(b_1, b_2) = \frac{\pi}{2} \left( \arccos \left( \frac{\|b_1\| \cdot \|b_2\|}{b_1 \cdot b_2} \right) - \frac{2\pi}{2} \right)^2.
\label{vb1b2}
\end{equation}
We use CIoU to assess the similarity between two bounding boxes, integrating position, size, and overlap.
%We employ CIoU to combine the position, size, and overlap of bounding boxes, allowing for an accurate assessment of the similarity between two bounding boxes.

%The detection head employs an anchor-based multi-scale detection scheme. It consists of three different scale feature layers, each corresponding to a different detection granularity. The finest-grained feature layer is used to detect small objects, while the coarsest-grained feature layer is used to detect large objects. On each feature layer, 1x1 and 3x3 convolutional layers are utilized to process the features, resulting in predictions of class probabilities and corresponding bounding boxes for each grid cell.

\subsubsection{Segmentation Head}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{segment_head_reset.pdf}
\caption{Structure of segmentation head. For objects of different scales, we utilize high-resolution features from the neck, passing multi-scale features to the pixel decoder.}
\vspace{-0.1cm}
\label{seghead}
\end{figure}

High-resolution features facilitate identifying objects at different scales, but they are computationally demanding. Therefore, we propose an efficient multi-scale strategy, as shown in \autoref{seghead}, that introduces high-resolution features while managing the computational load. Instead of consistently using high-resolution features, we utilize a feature pyramid that includes both low and high-resolution features.

Specifically, we use a feature pyramid generated by the pixel decoder at resolutions of 1/32, 1/16, and 1/8 of the original image. These embeddings are fed from the lowest to the highest resolution into the corresponding layers of the pixel decoder.


% To facilitate the reconstruction of high-resolution feature maps, our segmentation head resembles U-Net\cite{ronneberger2015u}'s design. It takes into account the diverse sizes and orientations of targets in images. To accomplish this, we employ skip connections to combine feature maps from various scales with the upsampled features along the segmentation head's path (\autoref{seghead}).

% %As shown in \autoref{seghead}, our segmentation head consists of upsampling, skip connections, and convolutional layers. 
% For each fusion scale, the feature maps are resized back to their original size by bilinear upsampling. Additionally, a $1\times1$ convolution is used to adjust the channel dimensions of feature maps from two neighboring scales.
%Due to the varying scales of houses and landing pads during UAV landing, it is necessary to analyze information at different scales. To address this issue, we perform upsampling fusion on the multi-scale feature layers of the detection head and use the detection task to constrain the target position in segmentation. This approach solves the problem of slow convergence and low accuracy in single segmentation tasks. Since the SPP (Spatial Pyramid Pooling) is shared in the feature fusion module, we do not add an additional SPP module to the segmentation branch. Additionally, we use bilinear interpolation in the upsampling layer instead of transposed convolution to reduce computational cost and eliminate the edge aliasing issues caused by nearest-neighbor interpolation. As a result, our decoder not only achieves high-precision outputs but also operates efficiently during inference.

%\subsection{Loss Function}
%Due to the presence of two detection heads in our network, our multi-task loss also consists of two components.


The segmentation loss is the weighted sum of Dice loss~\cite{2016V}, BCEWithLogits loss, and Focal loss. Since we are concerned with the boundaries and overall shapes of the UAV nest and houses,  the weight of the Dice loss is higher than other losses to increase the model's sensitivity to the target regions. %In the end, we set $\alpha_1$ =0.5, $\alpha_2$ =0.25, $\alpha_3$ =0.25.
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{seg}} = \alpha_1 \mathcal{L}_{\text{Dice}}+\alpha_2 \mathcal{L}_{\text{BCE}}+\alpha_3 \mathcal{L}_{\text{Focal}}
\end{equation}
After experimental testing, the best-performing parameters are $\alpha_1 = 0.5$, $\alpha_2 = 0.25$, and $\alpha_3 = 0.25$.
%Dice损失是一种常用的用于评估图像分割模型性能的损失函数。其优势在于它能够捕捉预测分割与实际分割之间的重叠部分，因此特别适用于处理边缘模糊问题。

We use Dice loss to evaluate the performance of image segmentation. Its advantage lies in capturing the overlap between predicted segmentation and ground truth segmentation, making it suitable for addressing edge ambiguity issues. 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{Dice}} = 1 - \frac{{2 \times TP}}{{2 \times TP + FP + FN}},
\label{lseg}
\end{equation}
where TP is the number of true positive pixels, FP is the number of false positive pixels, and FN is the number of false negative pixels.

However, the Dice loss focuses on the overall similarity between the predicted and ground truth masks, overlooking individual pixel values. Hence, in scenarios with only foreground and background, even a slight misprediction in small targets can cause a substantial fluctuation in Dice scores. 
To address this limitation, we integrate Dice loss with weighted cross-entropy loss by assigning weights to the small target regions. Additionally, considering the imbalance between classes, we introduce class weights in the loss computation to manage the disparity among different classes as follows:
\begin{align}
\mathcal{L}_{BCE}(y, \hat{y}) = -\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \biggl[ \frac{N_{\text{total}}}{2 \cdot N_{\text{positive}}} \cdot y_i \cdot \log(\hat{y}_i) +\\ 
\frac{N_{\text{total}}}{2 \cdot N_{\text{negative}}} \cdot (1 - y_i) \cdot \log(1 - \hat{y}_i) \biggr],\notag
\label{bce}
\end{align}
where $\hat{y}_i$ is the output, , and $y_i$ is the true label.


%通过结合Dice损失，BCEWithLogits损失和QFocal损失，我们可以考虑像素级相似性和整体分类准确性两个方面。Dice损失衡量了分割的重叠度，BCEWithLogits损失处理像素级的分类准确性，而QFocal损失处理不同目标的重要性和难易程度。这种组合使我们能够考虑分割任务的不同方面，从而帮助模型更好地处理复杂场景中的目标以及提高模型的性能和稳健性。

By combining Dice loss, BCEWithLogits loss, and Focal loss, we can balance both pixel-level similarity and overall classification accuracy. Dice loss measures the overlap of the segmentation, BCEWithLogits loss handles pixel-level classification accuracy, and Focal loss deals with the importance and difficulty of different targets. 
