\section{Introduction}  
The ability of LoRaWAN technology to deliver multi-kilometer connectivity with ultra-low power consumption has positioned it as a leading protocol for large-scale Internet of Things (IoT) deployments \cite{obiriSurveyLoRaWANIntegratedWearable2024c, fahmidaRTPLRealTimeCommunication2024}. While its physical layer (LoRa) excels in coverage, its utility for indoor localization, critical for applications like warehouse inventory tracking, hospital equipment monitoring, and smart factory workflows, remains constrained. The received signal strength indicator (RSSI)-based localization in LoRaWAN networks typically achieves \(8\text{–}20\,\mathrm{m}\) accuracy \cite{rathnayakeRSSIMachineLearningBased2023}, insufficient for fine-grained tracking where sub-\(10\,\mathrm{m}\) precision is required \cite{guoILLOCInHallLocalization2022}. This limitation arises from signal attenuation dynamics unique to indoor environments, including multipath interference from reflective surfaces, absorption losses due to humidity fluctuations, and transient obstructions (e.g., personnel or equipment) \cite{syazreenahmadRecentAdvancesWSNBased2024, weiRSSIbasedLocationFingerprint2024}.


Traditional path loss models, such as the COST 231 multi-wall indoor attenuation model \cite{europeancommissionCOSTAction2311999}, approximate signal decay as a function of distance and static wall penetration losses. While these provide a foundational framework, they fail to account for dynamic environmental factors \cite{azevedoCriticalReviewPropagation2024}. For instance, empirical studies demonstrate that temperature and humidity variations can alter the RSSI due to water vapor absorption at 868 MHz \cite{cattaniExperimentalEvaluationReliability2017}, \cite{lavdasEffectTemperatureHumidity2021}. At the same time, mobile obstacles like human beings subtly modify diffraction patterns \cite{grubelDenseIndoorSensor2022}. Such effects introduce non-stationary noise that classical models treat as Gaussian shadowing, leading to systematic localization errors in real-world deployments.


Recent advances employ machine learning (ML) to map RSSI patterns to spatial coordinates. Random forests and convolutional neural networks (CNNs) achieve \(5\text{–}7\,\mathrm{m}\) accuracy by learning complex relationships between RSSI, signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), and environmental variables \cite{rathnayakeRSSIMachineLearningBased2023}, \cite{voAdvancePathLoss2024a}. However, these models tend to operate as black boxes, obscuring the physical mechanisms driving signal attenuation. In safety-critical scenarios (e.g., tracking defibrillators in hospitals), the inability to diagnose why a model mislocates a device undermines trust and compliance. Furthermore, ML methods often require retraining when deployed in new environments, limiting scalability.


Our proposed work bridges these gaps by unifying physics-based propagation models with data-driven calibration. Building on the COST 231 Multi-Wall Model (\textbf{MWM}) framework \cite{europeancommissionCOSTAction2311999}, we extend the log-distance model to explicitly incorporate Environmental Parameter dynamics (temperature, relative humidity, carbon dioxide (CO\textsubscript{2}), particulate matter (PM\textsubscript{2.5}), and barometric pressure) and LoRa-specific parameters (RSSI, frequency, and SNR) (\textbf{MWM-EP}). Unlike many black-box-like ML models, our multiple linear regression (MLR) model quantifies the contribution of these factors. For example, by comparing the extended model with a baseline that only uses distance and wall-related parameters, we observed an improvement in R\textsuperscript{2} from \(0.6917\) to \(0.8222\), indicating that the additional parameters explain approximately \(13\%\) more variance in path loss. This interpretability enables network operators to pinpoint attenuation sources (e.g., elevated PM\textsubscript{2.5} levels in a densely occupied indoor conference center degrading signals) and adjust deployments accordingly.


We deploy a per-device forward‑only, innovation‑based Kalman filter \cite{kalmanNewApproachLinear1960} on the MWM-EP to mitigate RSSI volatility as proposed in \cite{liaoDynamicSelfTuningMaximum2022}, resulting in the \textbf{MWM-EP-KF} formulation. It distinguishes transient noise, such as fleeting human movement, collisions, or interference, from persistent path loss changes caused by environmental factors (heating, ventilation, and air conditioning), HVAC-induced temperature, and relative humidity shifts. Unlike traditional moving-average filters, which apply uniform weighting to all samples within a fixed window and introduce latency in dynamic environments, a Kalman filter dynamically tunes its process noise covariance based on real-time signal stability \cite{bultenKalmanFiltersExplained2015}. This adaptability enables precise separation of transient disturbances from sustained environmental shifts, a capability validated by recent advancements in adaptive filtering. For instance, hybrid frameworks like the Fourier-Transform Fuzzy-C-Means Kalman Filter (FFK) \cite{tsengFFKFourierTransformFuzzycmeans2023} demonstrate superior noise suppression in non-Gaussian RSSI distributions compared to static averaging methods, achieving \(8\%\) lower accumulated errors in dynamic indoor scenarios. Similarly, recursive Kalman-based approaches have proven robust against lag and outliers by prioritizing uncertainty-weighted updates over rigid windowed averaging\cite{bultenKalmanFiltersExplained2015}.

The paper’s contributions are threefold:
\begin{enumerate}  
  \renewcommand{\labelenumi}{\textbf{\textit{(\roman{enumi})}}}
  \item \textit{\textbf{Open Dataset and Analysis}}: A public LoRaWAN dataset and analysis of LoRaWAN packets (with metadata) synchronized with environmental sensor readings, collected across seasons in a real-world office setting; available at: \href{https://github.com/nahshonmokua/EnviKal-Loc}{\textcolor{blue}{\texttt{https://github.com/nahshonmokua/EnviKal-Loc}}}.
  \item \textit{\textbf{Indoor Environmental-Aware Path Loss and Shadowing (PLS) Modeling}}: A first-principles model augmented with environmental sensor data, achieving \(5.81\,\mathrm{m}\) MAE in distance estimation. Coefficients for the MLR model parameters are derived from six months of indoor real-world office measurements
  \item \textit{\textbf{Self-Tuning Kalman Filter}}: Device-specific, forward-only Kalman filter for adaptive RSSI smoothing that reduces localization volatility (\(\sigma\)  from \(9.95\,\mathrm{dBm}\) to \(5.28\,\mathrm{dBm}\)) without prior noise statistics.

\end{enumerate}


\section{EnviKal-Loc: Design and Methodology}

\subsection{Data Collection Campaign}

Our data measurement collection was conducted over a six-month indoor measurement campaign (mid-fall to mid-winter), using a single LoRaWAN gateway (GW) and six static end nodes (ENs). They were deployed in a typical office environment on the eighth floor (Figure.~\ref{fig: floor_plan}) of an academic building at the University of Siegen, Germany. This layout ensured a range of signal paths from unobstructed to heavily obstructed, emulating realistic indoor scenarios. The devices were distributed within a \( 40\,\mathrm{m} \) radius of the GW at a uniform height and placed to introduce varying propagation conditions. One EN-GW link was in line-of-sight (LoS), while others traversed multiple brick/concrete walls; non-line-of-sight (NLoS).
\begin{figure}[hbt!]
    \centering
    \centerline{\includegraphics[width=.9\linewidth]{Figures/snd_floor_plan.drawio.png}}
    \caption{\label{fig: floor_plan} Sensor network deployment layout showing sensor end nodes (EN1–EN6) and the gateway (GW) placement.}
    \Description{The Floor Plan of our Deployment.}
\end{figure}

Each EN was housed in a custom 3D-printed enclosure with a vertically oriented omnidirectional antenna (Figure.~\ref{fig: end_device}) and incorporated a suite of co-located environmental sensors, including the Sensirion SCD41 for CO\(_2\) concentration measurement, the Bosch BME280 for temperature, relative humidity, and barometric pressure levels, and the Sensirion SPS30 for PM\(_{2.5}\) particulate concentration. The LoRaWAN nodes were built on Arduino MKR WAN 1310 boards, transmitting at \( 868 \,\mathrm{MHz} \) with \( 20 \,\mathrm{dBm} \) output power and adhering to the \( 1\% \) duty cycle limit. Each device sent an \( 18 \,\mathrm{byte} \) uplink packet to the GW every \( 60 \,\mathrm{s} \), embedding sensor readings and a sequence counter in a compact binary format. To complete the network, a Kerlink Wirnet iFemtoCell indoor GW was configured for the EU868 band and provided robust indoor coverage with high receiver sensitivity (down to \( -141 \,\mathrm{dBm} \)). It forwarded all received EN packets to a cloud-hosted network server (The Things Network), relaying the data via MQTT to an AWS-hosted InfluxDB time-series database. 
