The performance by using \texttt{TaylorDiff.jl}~\cite{tan2022taylordiff,tan2022thesis} was between that of the approximate LW procedure and \texttt{Enzyme.jl} and is not shown to save space.
We also obtain similar results on other CPUs, e.g., an Apple M3 CPU.

\section{Conclusions}

The numerical results show that the LW scheme with AD is more robust than the approximate LW procedure, without losing out on performance.
The AD approach is also simpler to implement because it does not require different finite difference approximations for different orders of accuracy, as we demonstrated by extending the LW method to the sixth order of accuracy.
The following questions are open for future research.
LW with AD needs to be tested for other equations including different conservation laws, but also equations with source terms~\cite{babbar2024generalized} and parabolic terms~\cite{babbar2024parabolic}.
When computing higher-order derivatives, some computations from lower-order derivatives can be reused, although this is not done in the current implementation (Algorithm~\ref{alg:ad}).
A hard-coded implementation of the LW scheme using the chain rule could reuse these intermediate results to avoid redundant operations.
However, it is not clear whether this potential optimization is possible for the proposed LW-AD scheme.

