Some models of speech generation map a compact latent space of latent variables to a high-dimensional acoustic signal. In generative CNNs a fully-connected layer is used to project the latent space to a feature-map format that is then up-sampled in subsequent convolutional layers. While this division of labor between the fully-connected layer and subsequent convolutional layers is well understood, it has not been thoroughly explored from the standpoint of cognitive modeling. This study presents a technique for investigating what patterns are encoded uniquely in the convolutional layers. In experiment 1, we show I show that drastically shrinking the number of channels in the fully-connected layer produces linguistically interpretable outputs. We present an experiment testing whether outputs generated with random FC layer feature maps violate learned input data, and suggest that convolutional layers can be used to model phonotactics that enforce well-formed ness while the more computationally powerful fully-connected layer is equipped to model word-formation. 

This paper shows that when the number of output features in the fully-connected layer is reduced from 16,384 to 124, a WaveGAN successfully generates outputs consistent with the vowel harmony or disharmoy pattern it was trained on . We then show that inputting random feature maps into the convolutional layers produces linguistically-interpretable outputs which reflect what the convolutional layer learns independently of the FC-layer weights. We argue that having the FC layer constrain the range of outputs from a subspace of phonotactically well-formed outputs, is advantageous both from the persepctive of model interpretability, and plausibility as a model of phonotactics. 


\subsection{Convolutional layers as models of phonotactics}
The current study highlights the potential of considering outputs of FC layer configurations independent of those that are constrained by its weights. In particular, the unstructured but well-formed outputs generated by the convolutional layers highlight that the constraints of convolutional layers may be appropriate for modeling phonotactic restriction. 
These results also bear on prior attempts to understand the nature of local and non-local dependencies in GANs. 

Unsupervised audio synthesis models like generative CNNs often rely on a fully-connected layer (FC) to project a low-dimensional set of latent variables to a high-dimensional feature map that is than fed to a block of convolutional layers to produce an output. In WaveGAN, the focus of the current study, this FC featuare map has dimensions 1024x16, with 1024 channels of 16-value time series. The FC-layer bridges the latent variables and subsequent convolutional layers, so understanding its role in encoding properties of the training input is essential for evaluating models like GANs are plausible models of phonological learning. 



The current study focuses on the generator in GAN-based models, specifically on how its fully-connected layer interacts with subsequent convolutional layers. Recent work has investigated how GANs learn linguistic representations from raw speech for linguistic phenomena from a variety of languages (e.g. \cite{begus22Interspeech}, \cite{chen2023exploring}, \cite{barman2024unsupervised}). These studies focus on how linguistic phenomena are represented in the latent space of generative models. While the aforementioned work has demonstrated the ability of networks to represent human linguistic patterns in non-trivial ways, a challenge of these models is that even with disentanglement in the latent space, it is difficult to disentangle lexical and phonetic or phonotactic learning. Specifically, it is unclear to what extent any observed phonotactic dependency is dissociable from the lexical or structural context of its ouptut. 


\subsection{Non-local dependencies in CNNs}
Language is replete with non-local dependencies. In phonology, non-local dependencies are less common but nevertheless frequent. Perhaps the most famous kind of non-local dependency is vowel harmony.  The current study follows prior work in attempting to use GAN-based models for learning harmony (\cite{begusCSL}, \cite{ray2024deciphering}). Transformer-based models and RNNs both enable learning of harmony in that their architecture does not make any a-priori prioritization of local or non-local phenomena. The output of CNNs, however, is constrained by the spatial or temporal structure of an input at any given layer. Thus, it is possible that in WaveGAN -based models non-local dependencies are ``hardcoded'' in the fully-connected layer. Conversely, convolutional layers may enforce vowel identity in the outputs of FC layer codes that would independently specify separate vowels. 

The training data are generated using Amazon Polly's neural TTS model. We generate XX unique CVCVC sequences across 4 unique voices from the Spanish voice options- two male and two female.  The harmony condition consists of CVCVC words where the vowels are either both /a/, like /sasak/ or both /i/ like /sitis/. The disharmony condition consists of CVCVC words where vowels always consists of one /i/ and one /a/ in either order, like /sasik/. We restrict the range of consonants to voiceless consonants {CONSONANTS LIST}, because voiced consonants be shorter in duration and have more vowel-to-vowel coarticulation, and the transition between voiced vowels and consonants can be ambiguous making segmentation of speech in potentially noisy outputs challenging.  

In simple terms, convolutional layers are temporally constrained, while fully-connected layers are not. As such, limiting the less-constrained Fully-connected layer is an appropriate starting point for enhancing the cognitive plausibility of GAN-based models.  

 \section{Discussion}
This study demonstrates that outputs of convolutional layers yield interpretable outputs when the latent space is bypassed and random FC-layer feature maps are randomly generated, but only for the smaller bottleneck models. Because convolutional layers are local, these outputs closely resemble the phonotactics of the language. Methodologically, this study also demonstrates that generative CNNs like WaveGAN can be successfully trained even when the fully-connected layer bottleneck is reduced from the original 1024 channels (16384 features) to only 8 channels (124 features). 

This paper shows preliminary evidence that the architecture of WAveGAN does not contribute to enforcing non-local dependencies, like harmony. Future work can exploit the set of techniques presented in this paper to rigorously probe the extent to which particular kinds of dependencies lend themselves to being modeled upasampling convolutional layers. 

Practically, where the acoustic quality of the output is not of primary importance, pruning models is useful for reducing the size of models. We show that the FC layer, which redundantly encodes information in 1024 feature maps, is a good place to start. Testing the effect of bottlneecks and model pruning is relevant to a vast array of frameworks beyond the generative CNNs considered in this paper. 

 \subsection{Cognitive Modeling and the Curse of Dimensionality}

An auxiliary finding of this paper is that models can produce linguistically-interpretable outputs with fewer than 1\% of the original number of parameters. In addition the 8-channel model yields highly interpretable outputs when 8x16 feature maps are input into the convolutional layers, which is not the case for random 1024x16 feature maps in the 1024-channel model. 

The ability of random feature maps in the small bottleneck models to produce interpretable outputs is possibly a consequence of the curse of dimensionality: a 16,384-dimensional space is much harder to sample from and thus it is unlikely that a random feature map will yield a well-formed output. The 124-dimensional space, however is much easier to sample from, and feature maps that the convolutional layer is exposed to in training likely comprise a larger portion of the multidimensional space, and thus the convolutional layers do not over-fit to a narrow subset of a high dimensional space.

 I contend that a model in which the FC layer constrains a lower dimensional sub-space of feature maps whose configurations readily generate linguistic outputs, as opposed to higher dimensional space that includes non-speech like outputs, offers a more plausible model of human speech generation. If the FC layer is to model computationally complex processes like selection of lexical items to be input to a speech-generating apparatus, it appears plausible any output should contain evidence of sub-lexical phonemic representations. 


 While CNNs have largely been replaced by more computationally powerful frameworks like RNNs and Transformers in modeling speech, they are appealing for cognitive modeling because they are efficient (CITATION) and constrained by locality, an assumption central to many models of phonological grammar (CITATIONS). Moreover the partitioning between a computationally powerful fully-connected layer, and a computationally limited sequence of convolutional layers mirrors the division between lexical processes and phonotactics. Producing random outputs using the generator can provide a window into whether the highly local convolutional layer learns phonotactic constraints that are enforced in a fully generalizable manner. 

 The current study explores the consequences of bypassing the fully-connected layer by instead generating random feature maps to use as inputs into the convolutional block. The values of the random feature map are sampled from a uniform distribution.
To validate the Conv-only  8ch model, I also train it on a separate dataset: 1760 Amazon polly CVCVC sequences. In the Conv-only  outputs, vowels exhibit significant pitch variation as evident in the outputs in FIG XX (SHOW SPECTROGRAM WITH PITCH LINES). This indicates that the FC layer is responsible for ensuring that words are generated in a particular voice.  .\footnote{\blue{For reviewers: Waveforms, textgrids, and script are available in supplemental materials.}}

