\begin{abstract}
Temporal graphs are graphs whose edges are labelled with times at which they are active. Their time-sensitivity provides a useful model of real networks, but renders many problems studied on temporal graphs more computationally complex than their static counterparts. To contend with this, there has been recent work devising parameters for which temporal problems become tractable. One such parameter is vertex-interval-membership width. Broadly, this gives a bound on the number of vertices we need to keep track of at any time in order to solve any of a family of problems. Our contributions are two-fold.  Firstly, we introduce a new parameter, tree-interval-membership-width, that generalises both vertex-interval-membership-width and several existing generalisations.  Secondly, we provide meta-algorithms for both parameters which can be used to prove fixed-parameter-tractability for large families of problems, bypassing the need to give involved dynamic programming arguments for every problem. We apply these algorithms to temporal versions of 
Hamiltonian path, matching, edge deletion to limit maximum reachability, and firefighting. 
% \footnote{For the purpose of open access, the author(s) has applied a Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license to any Author Accepted Manuscript version arising from this submission.}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Temporal graphs are useful for modelling networks which change over time in contexts such as communication~\cite{baker_gossips_1972}, epidemiology~\cite{enright_deleting_2021}, and transport~\cite{fuchsle_temporal_2022}. Allowing edges to appear and disappear results in many temporal problems being computationally complex, even on very restricted inputs~\cite{akrida_temporal_2021,mertzios_computing_2023}. One approach for tackling these problems is to find temporal graph parameters for which the problem becomes tractable: this is an active area of research. In particular, there exist many temporal analogues to static structural graph parameters such as temporal feedback edge number~\cite{haag_feedback_2022}, timed feedback vertex number~\cite{casteigts_finding_2021}, temporal neighbourhood diversity, temporal modular-width, temporal cliquewidth~\cite{enright_structural_2024}, and multiple temporal versions of treewidth~\cite{fluschnik_as_2020,mans_treewidth_2014}. 

Here, we discuss two parameters which restrict the function that assigns edges to sets of times and the temporal graph structure, but do not explicitly restrict the underlying graph. We begin with the parameter vertex-interval-membership width (VIM width), as defined by Bumpus and Meeks~\cite{bumpus_edge_2023}. Informally, this is the maximum number (over timesteps) of vertices that are within their \emph{active interval}: that is, the time interval spanning the time of the first active edge incident to that vertex and the last (inclusive).  For some problems, we can ignore vertices outside their active interval -- making VIM width useful.  The VIM width of a temporal graph is calculated by finding the VIM sequence, a decomposition into a bag at each time containing only the vertices in their active interval at that time. A formal definition of this parameter is given in Section~\ref{sec:existing}. Algorithms for problems which are fixed-parameter-tractable (fpt) with respect to VIM width usually take the form of a dynamic program over the VIM sequence chronologically (e.g. edge exploration \cite{bumpus_edge_2023} and counting temporal paths \cite{enright_counting_2023}). 
We abstract the properties of problems that make it possible to define a dynamic program over the VIM sequence in order to give a meta-algorithm for any problem with these properties.  
%Temporal problems which are likely to be in FPT with respect to VIM width have the property that we only need to keep track of what happens at vertices in their active intervals. For example, problems which ask questions of existence of temporal paths will likely have this property as a vertex must be in its active interval at time $t$ to be traversed by a path at that time. We build a meta-algorithm by exploiting this characteristic of temporal problems which are tractable with respect to VIM width. 
Namely, we give an algorithm for all problems which can be solved by describing the set of possible starting states, performing an efficient check on two states at consecutive times to see if we can transition from one to the following, and performing an efficient check on the final state to check if it is acceptable. We call such problems \emph{locally temporally uniform} and give several examples of how our meta-algorithm can be used to show inclusion in FPT with respect to VIM width.

Although VIM width and algorithms over the VIM sequence are relatively straightforward, the class of temporal graphs for which this parameter is small is quite restricted: i.e.~at each time, all but a small number of vertices must either have not yet been active or must never be active again. In a VIM sequence, all vertices in their active interval at a given time are in the same bag regardless of their graph-distance at that time. Motivated by capturing how the connected components in a temporal graph evolve, Christodoulou et al.~\cite{christodoulou_making_2024} introduce three generalisations of VIM width: $\leq$-connected-vertex-interval-membership width, $\geq$-connected-vertex-interval-membership width, and bidirectional connected-vertex-interval-membership width. Bidirectional connected-VIM width generalises the other two widths they introduce, which in turn generalise VIM width. 
% They define $\leq$- (respectively, $\geq$-) connected interval membership widths to be the cardinality of the largest bag in a decomposition where a bag at some time is the intersection of the vertices in the bag of the VIM sequence at that time and the vertices in a connected component of the static graph whose edges appear up to (respectively, from) the time assigned to the bag in the original temporal graph. Bidirectional connected interval membership width takes the minimum value over all times of the maximum of the bag of the VIM sequence, and the bags of $\leq$- and $\geq$-connected interval membership widths. 
Definitions of these parameters can be found in Section~\ref{sec:existing}. Christodoulou et al.~use these parameters to dynamically program over time by solving a sub-problem on the connected components of the underlying graph of the temporal subgraph induced by the edges up to (respectively, from) time $t$. They show \textsc{Temporal Firefighter} to be in FPT with respect to $\leq$-connected-VIM width. They leave open the question of inclusion of \textsc{Temporal Firefighter} in FPT with respect to $\geq$-connected-VIM width. We answer this by showing the problem remains NP-hard on graphs for which $\geq$-connected-VIM width is bounded.

Our new parameter, tree-interval-membership (TIM) width, generalises the parameters introduced by Christodoulou et al.~\cite{christodoulou_making_2024}, and Bumpus and Meeks~\cite{bumpus_edge_2023}. The definition is motivated by noting that, in many temporal problems, if two vertices are not in the same connected component at time $t$, they can be considered independently at that time.  A TIM decomposition of a temporal graph consists of a collection of bags, each of which is associated with a timestep, indexed by a directed tree; there may be many bags associated with each timestep.  If two vertices $u$ and $v$ are in the same connected component at time $t$, then they must belong to the same bag at time $t$; sometimes more than one connected component at a single time must be combined into one bag to ensure the indexing graph is a tree, since we further require that, for every vertex $v$ and time $t$, the node indexing the (unique) bag containing $v$ at time $t$ must have a directed edge to the node indexing the bag containing $v$ at time $t+1$.  As with the other parameters, the width of a TIM decomposition is the cardinality of the largest bag.  Constructing an algorithm directly on a TIM decomposition is likely to require a technically detailed dynamic program. We give a meta-algorithm that shortcuts this process by instead requiring only five efficient checks to show that the problem meets requirements for meta-algorithm application. Those checks are: that we have appropriate starting states, that we have appropriate finishing states, that the states at all other times in the temporal graph are valid when restricted, that we can transition from one state to the following for each connected component and time, and a check on internal state counters. We call such a problem \emph{component-exchangeable temporally uniform}: intuitively, this name derives from the observation that these are problems on which we can perform checks during the dynamic program component-wise independently at each timestep.



