    \caption{The decomposition of the temporal graph in Figure~\ref{fig:eg-tim} found by Algorithm~\ref{alg:TIMW} before any iterations of the while loop in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-while}.}
    \label{fig:eg-tim-alg}
\end{figure}
% \begin{restatable}[$*$]{lem}{timalgtime}
\begin{restatable}{lem}{timalgtime}
\label{lem:TIM-runtime}
    Algorithm~\ref{alg:TIMW} outputs a TIM decomposition in $O(n^4\Lambda^2 \phi)$ time where $\phi$ is the width of the decomposition.
\end{restatable}
\begin{proof}
    We show that Algorithm~\ref{alg:TIMW} yields a TIM decomposition of the input temporal graph by reference to the criteria in Definition~\ref{def:TIMW}. We begin by showing that, once the for loop in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-for-loop} terminates, the first three criteria of Definition~\ref{def:TIMW} hold. That is, the decomposition constructed by the first 5 lines of the algorithm is such that: for each vertex there exists a bag with each time label containing it; each time-edge is contained in a correctly-labelled bag; and, for all times $1\leq t\leq \Lambda$ and bags $B$ labelled $t-1$, there is an arc from $B$ to every bag labelled with $t$ such that their intersection is non-empty.

    Since we begin by adding each connected component of each snapshot into a bag labelled with the time at which the snapshot is taken, we guarantee that every vertex appears in exactly one bag labelled with each time and that every time-edge in the temporal graph appears in exactly one bag of the decomposition. Furthermore, by the requirements of line~\ref{alg:TIMW-if}, there are arcs from bags at time $t$ to time $t+1$ for all times $t$ when the bags have non-empty intersection. Therefore, there is exactly one bag with each vertex in at each time, and this set of bags must have an arc between consecutive times from the earlier to the later.

    We now continue by showing that: the while loop at line~\ref{alg:TIMW-while} (and thus, the algorithm) terminates; after each iteration of the loop, the resulting decomposition graph still has properties 1-3 of Definition~\ref{def:TIMW}; and when the algorithm terminates, the resulting decomposition is a tree.

    To remove the cycle $C$, the for loops in lines~\ref{alg:TIM-for-union} and~\ref{alg:TIM-for-arcs} take a pair of bags $B$, $B'$ in the cycle labelled with the same times, add the vertices of $B'$ to $B$, redirect the endpoints of arcs incident with $B'$ to $B$, and delete the bag $B'$. We note first that performing this on all such pairs in a cycle must destroy that cycle and cannot add any more. Secondly, since we add all vertices in $B'$ to $B$ and delete $B'$, the property that each vertex appears exactly once in a bag labelled with each time must still hold. Similarly, following an iteration of the loop at line~\ref{alg:TIMW-while}, it must still be true that every time-edge appears in exactly one bag of the decomposition. Since we replace the endpoint of any arc incident to the bag we delete in the loop in line~\ref{alg:TIM-for-arcs}, the arcs of the decomposition must keep the property that they go from bags labelled with time $t$ to bags with time $t+1$ with non-empty intersection.
    By our previous reasoning, the properties that each vertex is in exactly one bag, and the arcs of the decomposition are between consecutive bags with non-empty intersection imply that the subgraph of the decomposition induced by the bags containing any given vertex must be a directed path. Recall that the number of cycles in the decomposition constructed by Algorithm~\ref{alg:TIMW} decreases with each iteration of the while loop in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-while}. Therefore, the loop and algorithm terminate.

    We now show that the decomposition output by the algorithm is a tree. By our earlier reasoning, once the while loop in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-while} terminates, there are no cycles remaining in the decomposition graph. We now assert that the graph is connected following the for loop in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-for-loop}, and remains connected through the iterations of the loop in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-while}. We claim that, since we take a temporal graph whose underlying graph is connected, the decomposition constructed in the first for loop (line~\ref{alg:TIMW-for-loop}) is connected. We show the claim by induction on the shortest path between two vertices. For our base case, we consider two vertices $u$ and $v$ which are adjacent in $\mathcal{G}_{\downarrow}$. Then, there must be at least one time $t$ at which the edge between them is active. Therefore, they must be in the same bag $B$ at time $t$. Furthermore, there must be an undirected path from every bag containing either $v$ or $u$ to $B$. Thus there is an undirected path from every bag containing every vertex $v$ to every bag containing every vertex $u$ (and vice-versa). Now suppose, for induction, that for every pair $u$, $v$ of vertices at distance at most $d$ from one another in $\mathcal{G}_{\downarrow}$, there is an undirected path from every bag containing every vertex $v$ to every bag containing every other vertex $u$ in the decomposition constructed by the loop in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-for-loop} of Algorithm~\ref{alg:TIMW}. 

    Let $v'$ and $u'$ be a pair of vertices such that the distance from $v'$ to $u'$ in $\mathcal{G}_{\downarrow}$ is exactly $d+1$. Let $w$ be the vertex adjacent to $v'$ on this path. By the inductive hypothesis, there is a path in the decomposition from every bag containing $w$ to a every bag containing $u'$. What remains is to show that there is a path from a bag containing $v$ to every bag containing $w$. Since $v$ and $w$ are adjacent to one another in $\mathcal{G}_{\downarrow}$, there must also be a path $P'$ from every bag containing $v$ to every bag containing $w$. Since we require an undirected path in the underlying undirected graph of the decomposition constructed in the first part of the algorithm, we can simply concatenate these paths to find a path from any bag containing $v$ to any bag containing $u$.
    By induction, for $d\in\mathbb{N}$ and all pairs of vertices $u$, $v$ distance $d$ from one another in $\mathcal{G}_{\downarrow}$ there must be a path in the decomposition graph from all bags containing a $v$ to all bags containing $u$.
    Since the distance between any two vertices in $\mathcal{G}_{\downarrow}$ is finite, there must be an undirected path between any two bags in the decomposition constructed by the loop in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-for-loop}.

    We now show that the decomposition graph constructed by the algorithm remains connected through each iteration of the while loop beginning in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-while}. Suppose there is an undirected path $P$ traversing the bag $B(i')$ which is removed during an iteration of the loop. Then, since the neighbourhood of $B(i)$ following the deletion of $B(i')$ is a superset of the neighbourhood of $B(i')$ before the for loop in line~\ref{alg:TIM-for-union} is executed, $B(i')$ can be replaced with $B(i)$ in $P$, and the resulting sequence of bags is a path in the decomposition following the deletion of $B(i')$. Hence, the decomposition must remain connected.
    Thus, since the decomposition output by Algorithm~\ref{alg:TIMW} is connected and cycle-free, it must be a tree.

    To analyse the runtime of this algorithm, we consider it in parts. In the for loop beginning in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-for-loop}, the algorithm creates a bag containing each connected component in each snapshot. This takes $O(n^2\Lambda)$ time. The algorithm then adds arcs between bags at consecutive times. There are at most $n\Lambda$ bags, so this takes time $O(n^2\Lambda)$. This works by, for each element in each bag, looking up which bag that element appears in at the next time. We assume looking up which bag the vertex appears in at the next time takes at most $O(n)$ time, since there are at most $n$ bags to check. Finally, the algorithm looks for cycles in the underlying graph of the decomposition. This can be performed by DFS (in $O(n^2\Lambda)$ time). If a cycle is found, we must merge at most $\Lambda$ bags to remove it. Merging of one bag takes at most $O(\phi)$ time (adding at most $\phi$ vertices to the other bag, moving the endpoints of at most $2\phi$ arcs, and deleting the bag) where $\phi$ is the cardinality of the largest bag. Since the merging of bags to remove a cycle removes at least 2 arcs, and there are at most $O(n^2\Lambda)$ arcs in the decomposition graph, the while loop is executed at most $O(n^2\Lambda)$ times. This gives us a runtime of $O(n^4\Lambda^2 \phi)$ in total.
\end{proof}
\begin{observation}\label{obs:timw-first-last-connected}
    Any bag labelled with time $t=1$ or $\Lambda$ in a TIM decomposition output by Algorithm~\ref{alg:TIMW} must contain the vertices in a single connected component in $G_t$.
\end{observation}

\begin{restatable}{lem}{timalgcorrect}\label{lem:TIM-correctness}
    Algorithm~\ref{alg:TIMW} outputs a TIM decomposition of minimum width.
\end{restatable}
\begin{proof}
    We show this result by proving the intermediate claim that any TIM decomposition output by Algorithm~\ref{alg:TIMW} has the property that every bag of the decomposition must be entirely contained by a bag in any TIM decomposition. This then implies that the decomposition given by Algorithm~\ref{alg:TIMW} is of minimum width.

    We prove the claim by induction on the number iterations of the while loop in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-while} of the algorithm.
    Our base case occurs when there are no cycles in the decomposition output by the loop. By Observation~\ref{obs:tim-connected-component}, all connected components in each snapshot must be contained in a single bag labelled with the time of the snapshot. Therefore, in any TIM decomposition of the input graph, it would still need to have the property that connected components in a snapshot must be contained in a single bag. We note that this also implies that the decomposition constructed by the for loop beginning in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-for-loop} of the algorithm has the property that any two vertices in the same bag must be in the same bag of any TIM decomposition of the input graph.

    Now suppose that, for every temporal graph $\mathcal{G}$ such that the 
    while loop in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-while} of Algorithm~\ref{alg:TIMW} executes at most $c$ times the algorithm gives a TIM decomposition such that every bag of the decomposition must be entirely contained by a bag in any TIM decomposition. More specifically, our inductive hypothesis is that, after $c$ iterations of the while loop beginning at line~\ref{alg:TIMW-while}, the decomposition graph constructed by the algorithm retains the property that any two vertices in the same bag must be in the same bag of any TIM decomposition of the input graph.

    Suppose that, for a temporal graph $\mathcal{G}$, the algorithm executes the while loop in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-while} $c+1$ times. We recall that before we enter the while loop in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-while}, the constructed decomposition must have the property that any pair of vertices in the same bag must be in the same bag of any TIM decomposition. We aim to show that this property is retained throughout all executions of the while loop. By the inductive hypothesis, following $c$ executions of the while loop, the decomposition constructed $T'$ must have the property that any two vertices in a bag of $T'$ must be in the same bag of any TIM decomposition of the input temporal graph. 

    Let $v$ and $u$ be vertices in different bags of $T'$ that are in the same bag $B$ of the TIM decomposition output by the algorithm. Then $B$ must be in a cycle in $T'$. We note that any cycles in the decomposition found by the for loop in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-for-loop} must consist of a sink, a source and pairs of bags $B_1,B_2$ such that $\tau(B_1)=\tau(B_2)$. The while loop beginning in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-while} functions by merging all such pairs of bags. Therefore, there must be a second bag $B'$ such that the bag containing $v$ and $u$ in the TIM decomposition output by the algorithm is $B'\cup B$.

    Suppose that there exists a TIM decomposition of the input graph with graph $T^*$ such that $v$ and $u$ remain in different bags. Then, for $T^*$ to be a tree, the cycle containing $B$ cannot exist. By the inductive hypothesis, all bags in $T'$ must be a subset of a bag in $T^*$. If $T^*$ is a TIM decomposition such that $u$ and $v$ are not in the same bag at time $t=\tau(B)$, then they cannot be in the same bag at both times $t_1,t_2$, where $t_1<t<t_2$, $t_1$ is the time given to the source of the cycle by $\tau$ and $t_2$ is the time given to the sink. Note that this source is unique because all arcs go from a bag at one time to a bag at the next time. This implies that the bags at the source and sink of the cycle in $T'$ are not subsets of bags of $T^*$; a contradiction. Therefore, $u$ and $v$ must be in the same bag of any TIM decomposition of the input graph. As $u$ and $v$ were arbitrary vertices in bags of the cycle in $T'$, this must be true of all such pairs. Therefore, any two vertices in a bag of the decomposition following $c+1$ iterations of the while loop in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-while} must be in the same bag of any TIM decomposition of the input temporal graph. 

    Thus, the decomposition graph constructed by the algorithm retains the property that any two vertices in the same bag must be in the same bag of any TIM decomposition of the input graph through all iterations of the while loop in line~\ref{alg:TIMW-while}. Therefore, for every TIM decomposition $(T,B,\tau)$ output by Algorithm~\ref{alg:TIMW}, every bag of the decomposition must be entirely contained by a bag in any TIM decomposition. Hence, there can be no smaller TIM decomposition of $\mathcal{G}$.
\end{proof}
Combining Lemmas~\ref{lem:TIM-runtime} and~\ref{lem:TIM-correctness} gives Theorem~\ref{thm:tim-alg}.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:tim-alg}
    Given a temporal graph $\mathcal{G}$ with lifetime $\Lambda$ and $n$ vertices,
    we can find a TIM decomposition of minimum width $\phi$ in time $O(n^4\Lambda^2\phi)$.
\end{theorem}

