\begin{abstract}


Open source software development, particularly within institutions such as universities and research laboratories, is often decentralized and difficult to track. Despite producing highly impactful tools in science, these efforts often go unrecognized due to a lack of visibility and institutional awareness. This paper addresses the challenge of discovering, classifying, and analyzing open source software projects developed across distributed institutional systems. We present a framework for systematically identifying institutional affiliated repositories, using the University of California (UC) system as a case study. 

Using GitHub's REST API, we build a pipeline to discover relevant repositories and extract meaningful metadata. We then propose and evaluate multiple classification strategies, including both traditional machine learning models and large language models (LLMs), to distinguish affiliated projects from unrelated repositories and generate accurate insights into the academic open source landscape. Our results show that the framework is effective at scale, discovering over 52,000 repositories and predicting institutional affiliation with high accuracy.


\keywords{Open Source Software  \and Software classification.}
\end{abstract}
%
%
\section{Introduction}

Open source software plays a central role in modern software engineering as the foundation of critical infrastructure and scientific discovery. Despite its impact, understanding where and how open source is produced remains a challenge, particularly when it is developed by decentralized institutions such as universities and research laboratories. In these settings, development is often distributed across personal accounts, making it difficult to discover and attribute contributions to their source organizations. 

Institutions around the world are increasingly recognizing the need to discover and map their open source ecosystems. Efforts such as the Institutional Innovation Grapher at the University of Texas at Austin \cite{UTOSPOGrapher}, the OSPO Database Development Playbook at the University of Wisconsin \cite{GoringUWProject}, the Stanford Open Source Registry \cite{StanfordOSRegistry}, the Janelia OSSI initiative on the Howard Hughes Medical Institute Research Campus \cite{JaneliaOSSIProjects} and France's national research software catalog \cite{MinEduResearchCatalogue} demonstrate a growing interest in creating tools to discover, classify and showcase open source software within institutions. These initiatives aim to support researchers, improve digital infrastructure, and make institutional impact more visible.

Unlike software developed within companies or centralized teams, academic open source research emerges usually from small university groups or even individual researchers. There is often no unified platform, naming convention, or metadata standard for these efforts. Websites like GitHub are commonly used by researchers to develop code collaboratively, but the vast scale of these systems makes discovery an arduous challenge.

Several factors make the task of identifying institutionally affiliated repositories particularly difficult:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Contributor profiles do not always include standardized institutional information and, when they do, it can be inconsistent, incomplete, or ambiguous.
    \item Not all repositories represent meaningful contributions. Forks, class assignments, and inactive personal experiments are hard to filter from mature and widely used projects.
    \item Contributors may use multiple GitHub identities, switch institutions, or contribute in a personal capacity, making it difficult to trace contributions to a specific organization.
    \item Repository searches often yield tens of thousands of results per institutional keyword or phrase (e.g. "UC Davis"). Manual filtering is impractical, and simple keyword filters are insufficient.
\end{enumerate}

In this paper, we discuss methods to systematically discover and classify open source repositories affiliated with academic institutions. Our approach combines GitHub’s REST API with a filtering pipeline that integrates machine learning and large language models (LLMs) to infer repository affiliation.% and filter out noise.

This work is carried out in collaboration with the Open Source Program Office Network of the University of California (UC OSPO Network)\cite{uc-network}, a multicampus initiative launched in 2024 to support open source research, promote sustainability, and establish best practices in academic settings \cite{uc-network-grant}. We apply our methods at scale to analyze repositories across the ten UC campuses, illustrating the effectiveness of our approach in a large, distributed university system. Our contributions include,



\begin{itemize}
    \item We present a method to discover open source repositories affiliated with academic institutions and discuss the main challenges in determining institutional affiliation.
    \item We develop a framework for systematically classifying the discovered repositories by affiliation.
    \item We compare our methods with baseline approaches previously used in similar contexts and show that our approach improves the accuracy of identifying affiliated repositories.
    \item We apply our framework to a large public university system and provide insights into open source development practices across campuses.
    \item We release the code for repository scraping and classification as an open-source tool, enabling replication and further research. The code is available at: \url{https://github.com/UC-OSPO-Network/repofinder}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}

There is growing interest in discovering and classifying open source software repositories to better understand their purpose, usage, and practices. For \textbf{discovery}, some universities have developed tools such as the Institutional Innovation Grapher \cite{UTOSPOGrapher}, which identifies GitHub accounts that mention a university in their bios. However, this method has limitations, as many developers do not explicitly mention institutional affiliations. Similarly, the University of Wisconsin open source monitoring project \cite{GoringUWProject} aims to discover open source contributions but does not automate affiliation detection. Schwartz et al.\cite{schwartz2024} present a more comprehensive effort by combining GitHub keyword searches with university website scraping and LLM-based filtering to identify research software engineering (RSE) repositories; however, affiliation is not directly studied.

For \textbf{classification}, previous work has addressed several angles. One of the most common, is distinguishing software engineering projects from other non-research projects. Munaiah et al.\cite{Munaiah2017} implemented a tool called Reaper to classify engineered software projects using a combination of score-based heuristics and random forest classifiers. Pickerill et al.\cite{Pickerill2020} extended this idea with PHANTOM, a time-series clustering method based on Git logs. 
Schwartz et al.\cite{schwartz2024} also classified RSE projects using LLMs.

Many efforts focus on categorizing repositories by topic and functionality \cite{DiRocco2020}, \cite{DiSipio2020}, \cite{Wang2012} \cite{Widyasari2023}, \cite{izadi2021}. Sharma et al.\cite{Sharma2017} proposed automatic cataloging based on topic modeling of README files, while Zhang et al.\cite{zhang2021} used keyword-driven hierarchical classification to assign repositories to topic categories. Aslam et al.~\cite{Aslam2022} built a multilingual source code classifier using machine learning, and Linares-Vasquez et al.\cite{LinaresVasquez2014} classified software by application domain using (API) calls from third-party libraries. Embedding-based models have also been explored: Bharadwaj and Kadam\cite{Bharadwaj2022} applied BERT-style models to classify GitHub issues, while Izadi et al.\cite{Izadi2023} used semantic knowledge graphs to improve topic recommendation.

GitHub mining at scale has also been a focus of previous work. Kalliamvakou et al.\cite{Kalliamvakou2014} highlighted challenges in GitHub data quality and usage patterns. Ma et al.\cite{Ma2019} introduced World of Code, an infrastructure for mining global VCS data and exploring interdependencies. Cosentino et al.\cite{Cosentino2017} conducted a systematic mapping study of related GitHub research.

Although these works significantly advance methods for repository discovery and classification, they generally focus on project characteristics, topic modeling, or development activity, without directly addressing the question of institutional ownership or affiliation. This point is emphasized by a recent systematic mapping study~\cite{balla2025} that surveys 43 works on repository classification. The authors note that, while many papers cite "discoverability" as the main motivation, none explicitly tackles the problem of institutional affiliation, highlighting a clear research gap. Our work addresses this gap by developing a dedicated methodology for identifying institutionally affiliated repositories across decentralized academic systems. We build on and extend previous discovery and classification methods, with a novel emphasis on affiliation detection and institutional mapping.




\section{Data Collection}
As the starting point for our data collection, we focus on GitHub because of its dominance in open source development, extensive API support, and widespread adoption across academic institutions (we plan to extend our work to other platforms in future work). %GitHub's REST API \cite{github-api} enables structured queries across key repository fields such as name, description, and README. 
To clarify our data collection and classification process, we first summarize the basic structure of a GitHub repository and the types of metadata available through the GitHub API. A \textbf{repository} is the central unit of collaboration on GitHub, containing a codebase, documentation, and configuration files. Each repository includes a number of descriptive \textbf{attributes}, such as its name, description, README file, and associated tags. Repositories may also include \textbf{community files}, like a code of conduct, contributing guidelines, and security policies, files that tend to signal project maturity and openness to external contributors. \textbf{Engagement indicators} such as stars, forks, downloads, and subscriber count reflect how active and visible a project is within the open source ecosystem. Furthermore, each repository is owned by an individual or an \textbf{organization} (a special GitHub account for groups), and includes a list of \textbf{contributors} who have committed code, often with metadata such as their username, affiliation, or location. 


To guide our analysis, we structure the data collection process into three distinct phases. Phase 1: Repository Data, where we collect core metadata and community files from GitHub repositories; Phase 2: Contributor Data, which enriches the dataset with information about individual contributors; and Phase 3: Organization Data which captures additional information from repository owners classified as organizations in GitHub. Figure~\ref{fig:scraping_diagram} provides an overview of this multi-phase workflow, highlighting the specific GitHub API endpoints and data elements involved in each stage.


