\subsubsection{Phase 1: Repository Data}

The goal of this phase is to identify all repositories that might be associated with an academic institution among all those available on GitHub.  We begin by constructing a high-recall seed list of lexical cues that denote University of California (UC) campuses. Because this step deliberately favors recall over precision, we retain all matches and defer the removal of false positives to the classification section.

 To identify open source repositories affiliated with University of California campuses, we gather a set of institution-specific search \textbf{keywords:} University \texttt{name, acronym, domain,} and a common \texttt{alternate name} (e.g. "UC Berkeley") as shown in Table \ref{tab:uc-campuses}. We then use these keywords to query GitHub’s repository search endpoint \texttt{/search/repositories} by matching them against five repository \textbf{attributes}: \texttt{name, description, README, tags} and \texttt{email}. For the first four attributes (\texttt{name}, \texttt{description}, \texttt{README}, and \texttt{tags}), we search using the four keywords, resulting in 4 keywords × 4 attributes = 16 queries. For the \texttt{email} attribute, however, we only search using the university domain, generating a single additional query. This results in a total of 17 queries per campus.

