\subsubsection{Method 2: Text Embeddings \& Machine Learning}

%We next develop a supervised machine learning method that  leverages text embeddings to classify repositories. 

Instead of relying only on keyword matches, our next approach creates a feature vector by using an embedding of all the meta-data of the repository, and then uses that feature vector as an input to a machine-learning classifier. Notice that the embedding captures the broader context in which university-related terms appear. For example, the phrase “developed by UC Santa Cruz” carries much stronger affiliation than an incidental mention of the campus name.

\noindent \textbf{Embedding:} We aggregate the repository attributes into a single text block. 

For each repository \( r_i \), we define:
$
\mathbf{m}_i = \left( \text{repo}_i, \text{org}_i, \text{contrib}_i^{(1)}, \text{contrib}_i^{(2)} \right),
$
where each component is a set of textual fields. We concatenate these fields into a single raw text string \( t_i \in \mathbb{T} \), such that $t_i = \text{concat}(\mathbf{m}_i).$ We finally transform each \( t_i \) into a vector representation \( \mathbf{v}_i \in \mathbb{R}^d \) using OpenAI's text embedding model \cite{openai2024embeddings} \( \mathcal{E} : \mathbb{T} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^d \).  Given the input size constraints of the small embedding model, we truncate each text field to a maximum of 10,000 characters. Since the README is typically the longest field, this truncation primarily affects its content.


\noindent \textbf{Training:} We create a random subset of repositories and assign a binary manual label to each of them \( y_i \in \{0, 1\} \), where \( y_i = 1 \) indicates affiliation with a university. 

We tested several machine learning models, including neural networks, random forests, and Support Vector Machines (SVM). The classification results were very similar among all models, but SVMs gave slightly better results, so we showcase them for the rest of this paper. 
After training, for each repository, the model outputs a probability per class:
$
p_i = f_{\text{prob}}(\mathbf{v}_i),
$
where \( p_i \) is the predicted probability that \( r_i \) is affiliated with the target university.

This approach requires a manually labeled training dataset, which introduces significant overhead in terms of human effort and time. To address this limitation, we next explored using large language models, which avoid the need for manual labeling. This approach is described in the following section.

\subsubsection{Method 3: Large Language Models}
For our last approach, we leverage large language models (LLMs), specifically ChatGPT versions \texttt{gpt-3.5-turbo} and \texttt{gpt-4o}, to classify repositories for university affiliation. The classification approach involves querying ChatGPT with a prompt that provides detailed contextual information about each repository and the campus-related keywords along with our definition of what it means for a repository to be "affiliated" to a university.

The prompt asks the model to return a probability value between 0 and 1 indicating the likelihood that the repository is affiliated with the university, along with a brief explanation justifying its assessment. To create the prompt, we aggregate multiple metadata components: repository metadata \( \text{repo}_i \), organizational metadata \( \text{org}_i \), and information about the top contributors \( \text{contrib}_i^{(1)} \) and \( \text{contrib}_i^{(2)} \). These are the same attributes used in our previous method. Each field is truncated to 20,000 characters because of the token input limit of the model. %Additionally, the prompt includes university specific keywords as defined earlier.
An example of the constructed prompt is as follows:

\begin{adjustwidth}{1em}{1em}
\footnotesize
\ttfamily
You are tasked with determining the likelihood that a GitHub repository belongs to a university based on the following definition:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Definition: <Definition 1>
    \item Here is the information about the repository: <repository information>
    \item And here is the university context: <university information> 
\end{itemize}

\noindent % r
Based on this information and the definition above: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item Estimate the probability between 0 and 1 (e.g., 0.87) representing how likely it is that the repository belongs to the university.
    \item Provide a brief explanation (1-2 sentences) justifying your answer.
\end{itemize}
Your response must be formatted exactly like this:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{Probability: <value between 0 and 1>}
\item \texttt{Explanation: <your explanation here>}
\end{itemize}


\end{adjustwidth}


