\subsubsection{Model Evaluation:}

Figure \ref{fig:roc_all} shows the models' ROC curves for UCSB, UCSC, and UCSD along with the corresponding AUC values.

\begin{figure}[htb]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1\textwidth]{roc_combined.png}
  \caption{ROC curves for UCSB, UCSC, and UCSD for the four models evaluated. \texttt{gpt-4o} achieves the highest area under the curve (AUC) for two of the three campuses, indicating strong overall classification performance.}
  \label{fig:roc_all}
\end{figure}

It is clear that the SBC model performs poorly across all three campuses, with ROC curves that closely follow the diagonal, suggesting the model is unable to effectively distinguish between the classes. This is because simple keyword matching is insufficient to determine repository affiliation. The context in which university-related terms appear is critical: for example, two repositories may mention a university the same number of times, but one may do so in a meaningful attribution (e.g., an affiliated lab) while the other only cites a resource or course. SBC fails to distinguish such cases, assigning low probabilities to affiliated repositories like \citeR{repo:theia}, \citeR{repo:shellnoob}, \citeR{repo:kbgan}, \citeR{repo:mojitalk}, \citeR{repo:charitably}, and \citeR{repo:c-sources}, where the only mention is subtle, like “built at [university]", “our lab at [university website]”, "contact me at [university email]". On the other hand, it assigns high probabilities to unaffiliated repositories like \citeR{repo:awesome-gnn}, \citeR{repo:pentest-bookmarks}, and \citeR{repo:awesome-autonomous}, which simply compile lists of resources and contain multiple university name matches. This highlights the main weakness of SBC: it lacks contextual understanding, making it unreliable as a classifier for affiliation.

Looking at the AUC score, \emph{on average} \texttt{gpt-4o} performs better than other classifiers, but no single model consistently outperforms the others across all campuses (\texttt{gpt-4o} is not the top classifier in the UCSB dataset). This suggests that model performance is sensitive to the characteristics of the university dataset. UCSD, in particular, shows worse results compared to UCSB and UCSC. First, UCSD has a significantly larger dataset: over 10,000 repositories collected (with 5,800 unique) and more than 11,000 contributors, making the classification task inherently more complex. Second, while inspecting the test dataset we find that among the positive cases, fewer repositories are clearly owned by organizations directly affiliated with the university. Many projects appear to be collaborative efforts with other institutions such as MIT (\citeR{repo:torchsparse}), Stanford (\citeR{repo:partnet_dataset}), or industry partners like Facebook (\citeR{repo:resnext}) and Adobe (\citeR{repo:diffusion_rig}). Others involve multiple institutions (\citeR{repo:slang}), making institutional attribution even more challenging. On the negative class, almost all the UCSD repositories from the test set consist primarily of aggregated educational resources (e.g., papers, courses, tools), while UCSC has more varied negative samples, including research artifacts like \citeR{repo:atacworks} that are genomics-related but not clearly affiliated with UCSC, or repositories from other institutions with the same acronym (e.g., \citeR{repo:fossalgo} from the University of Colombo School of Computing). UCSB’s dataset includes related software libraries (\citeR{repo:sortedcontainers}), citation references (\citeR{repo:addchain}), and cloud tools (\citeR{repo:rightaws}). This broader diversity in the negative class for UCSB and UCSC likely improves the classifier performance and the lack of variation in UCSD makes it hard to learn how to distinguish between "affiliated" and "non-affiliated" repositories.


\subsection{Discussion}


Looking directly at the predictions made by each model, we observe differences in their behavior, particularly in edge cases where it is difficult to determine affiliation. For example, several repositories are correctly classified by SVM but incorrectly by both \texttt{gpt-3.5-turbo} and \texttt{gpt-4o}. These are mostly false negatives, repositories that should be marked as affiliated but are missed by the language models. Examples include \citeR{repo:openfermion}, \citeR{repo:openfermion-pyscf}, \citeR{repo:design-patterns-julia}, \citeR{repo:c-specialization}, and \citeR{repo:vsdsram}. In many of these cases, the connection to the university is subtle, such as a professor mentioned at the end of the README, or brief one line acknowledgments that require careful attention. The language models struggle especially when there are multiple contributors from multiple institutions. This suggests that when the relevant information is difficult to find, language models are more likely to miss it.

On the other hand, GPT-based models, especially \texttt{gpt-4o}, do a better job avoiding false positives. These are cases where SVM incorrectly predicts a university affiliation, but both \texttt{gpt-3.5-turbo} and \texttt{gpt-4o} get it right. For example, repositories like \citeR{repo:addchain}, \citeR{repo:learn-physics}, \citeR{repo:awesome-neuro}, \citeR{repo:view-ast-tree}, and \citeR{repo:lstools} are identified by GPT models as unaffiliated, even though they mention a university or include a paper citation. In these cases, the models correctly pick up on context: the university may be cited due to dependencies, references or predecessor programs, but it is not actually involved with the project. This reflects the LLMs’ advantage in understanding context and complex relationships which is evidenced in the greater performance for UCSD.

Comparing \texttt{gpt-3.5-turbo} and \texttt{gpt-4o}, we find that \texttt{gpt-4o} often makes better decisions on more challenging examples. For instance, in cases like \citeR{repo:ictf-framework}, \texttt{gpt-4o} shows better affiliation judgment where the relationship is not explicit but having just a contact email with the university domain is enough indication. However, it is not flawless. In a few cases, such as \citeR{repo:awesome-text-to-video}, \citeR{repo:open-flamingo}, and \citeR{repo:profile-info},  its predictions seem overly cautious (following the definition too strictly) or based on hallucinated affiliations (confusing universities).

Some repositories are hard to classify for all models. One of the biggest issues we run into is the length of README files. In some cases, the affiliation signals, like a university mention or contributor info, appear at the very end, like in \citeR{repo:diffusion-rig}, \citeR{repo:diffusion-rig}, and \citeR{repo:3d-bat}. Since SVM, \texttt{gpt-4o} and \texttt{gpt-3.5-turbo} models can only process a limited amount of text at a time, this information often gets cut off. We try extending the input length for \texttt{gpt-4o}, which supports more tokens, but that actually makes things worse; the extra text adds noise, and the model starts making less accurate predictions. Therefore, we maintain the same truncation as in \texttt{gpt-3.5-turbo}.

Examples of repositories that all models misclassify as “affiliated” include \citeR{repo:fun-with-computer-graphics}, a collection of university lab resources where terms like "labs" and "resources" appear frequently, creating misleading signals, and \citeR{repo:gpgpu-sim_distribution}, which mentions a dependency of the project which is created by the university, making the interpretation challenging. Others like \citeR{repo:Data_Anaytics_UCSC}, on the other hand, are missed even though it mentions UC Santa Cruz in the description, but there is not enough structural evidence like contributor affiliation or sponsorship.

These examples highlight the challenges of automated affiliation identification.



