\caption{Predicted affiliated repositories per university using \texttt{gpt-4o}.}
\label{tab:predictions}
\end{table}

With these predictions, we can get insights about the types of practices and trends of university-affiliated repositories. 
We start by analyzing the programming languages and software licenses used in the affiliated repositories. Given space constraints, we present detailed results for three universities as representative examples.

\begin{figure}[htb]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=.8\textwidth]{language_distribution.png}
    \caption{Programming language distribution across UCSB, UCSC, and UCSD repositories.}
    \label{fig:language-distribution}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:language-distribution} shows the distribution of languages used in the repositories of each university.  The language distribution varies by university, with Python being the most common language at UCSB and UCSC, while Java is the top language at UCSD. JavaScript is used much less at UCSD compared to UCSB and UCSC. These differences may reflect the main research and teaching areas at each university; for example, a stronger emphasis on software engineering or systems programming at UCSD could explain the higher use of Java.

\begin{figure}[htb]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=.8\textwidth]{license_distribution.png}
    \caption{Software license usage across UCSB, UCSC, and UCSD repositories.}
    \label{fig:license-distribution}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:license-distribution} shows the distribution of software licenses. One of the most notable trends is that more than 60\% of the repositories do not have an explicit license across all campuses, with UCSB and UCSC both exceeding 70\%. Among repositories that include a license, the most common ones are MIT, Apache, and GPL, with MIT being the most used. The high proportion of unlicensed repositories may reflect the nature of the projects: many could be class assignments, course materials, or small student projects, which are not typically licensed. In contrast, tools and research software, which are more likely to have a license, make up a smaller part of the dataset. 


\begin{figure}[htb]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=.8\textwidth]{feature_counts.png}
    \caption{Presence of community files across UCSB, UCSC, and UCSD repositories.}
    \label{fig:community-standards}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:community-standards} illustrates the percentage of repositories that include GitHub’s recommended community standards \cite{github-guidelines}, such as a description, README, license, code of conduct, contributing guide, security policy, issue templates, and pull request templates. These elements suggest how well a project supports community engagement and maintainability beyond just the code \cite{github_community_tools}. Including these files is considered a best practice in open source development, as they help make a project easier to understand, use, and contribute to.

 In general, most repositories include a description and README. Only about 30\% per university have an explicit license, but when looking at the other community guidelines, the percentages reduce considerably. Less than 2\% of the repositories provide contributing guidelines, codes of conduct, or templates for issues and pull requests. With regard to security policy, it is clear that only a handful of projects actually have one. This trend suggests that most of the repositories are small-scale efforts, rather than mature open source tools that expect outside contributions. It may also reflect a general lack of awareness among students and researchers about open source best practices, even though they are essential to enabling collaboration, reuse, and long-term sustainability.

There is a clear gap and an opportunity for improvement: increasing awareness and helping project maintainers navigate these practices could strengthen the open source culture within academic settings.

\section{Conclusions}
In this work, we introduced a framework for identifying and classifying open source repositories affiliated with decentralized institutions. Our approach addresses the major challenges around attribution and the high level of noise present in large-scale repository datasets. The results show that a significant portion of the repositories returned by our initial queries are not actually affiliated with the target institutions. This highlights the importance of using automated classification methods to filter out noise, as it would not otherwise be possible to perform a large-scale analysis given the amount of unrelated data.

One of the main limitations of our approach is the GitHub API cap of 1,000 repositories per query. As a result, our dataset misses affiliated repositories, especially for larger institutions.  We took efforts to mitigate this limitation by decomposing broad queries into multiple narrower ones: one query per keyword per repository field (e.g., name, description, README). Although this strategy improved coverage, expanding it further may require more sophisticated search methods or integrating additional data sources.

Another limitation is the difficulty of evaluating accuracy at scale. Manual labeling is time-consuming, and given the size of the dataset, we were only able to validate predictions for a small subset of universities. 


\textbf{Ethical Considerations}: Only public-facing metadata was collected; no private repositories or user data were accessed. All data collection is in accordance with the GitHub Terms of Service.

