Unlike the ROC curves, where the relative performance of the model is less clear, Table 4 shows that \texttt{gpt-4o} consistently performs better than \texttt{gpt-3.5-turbo}. We also added a seed to the \texttt{gpt-4o} model (a feature not supported in \texttt{gpt-3.5-turbo}), which allows for reproducible results. SVM performs competitively, particularly at UCSB, but its performance drops at UCSD, where \texttt{gpt-4o} achieves the highest accuracy (0.84). This suggests that \texttt{gpt-4o} may be better suited for handling more complex or noisier datasets. 

\subsection*{Cost and Runtime Comparison}

Given the complexity of detecting affiliated repositories, it is also important to consider the practical trade offs in cost and runtime across different modeling approaches. Table \ref{tab:model-costs-times-52k} shows a cost comparison for different methods of processing GitHub repository data. For the SVM approach the cost reflects transforming the text into vector embeddings. The total time is computed by adding the embeddings time to the model training and prediction time, which in most cases is around 0.70 seconds. For the other methods, costs are based on using the OpenAI API with prompts whose length includes both dynamic repository data and a static prompt component. We compute average input sizes by concatenating all relevant data fields, resulting in approximately 2,900 characters of dynamic text plus about 1,600 characters of static prompt text for the API models. The cost estimates are scaled to 52K repositories, which corresponds to the total number of repositories found for the UC System.

