    \caption{The base LLM undergoes continued pre-training (CPT) on documents and supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on SFT data pairs to enhance query rewriting}
    \label{fig:methods_overview}
\end{figure}

This paper studies the rewrite step in the Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pipeline. So, we briefly introduce the RAG pipeline~\cite{ma2023query} here. Usually, we have a document corpus $\mathcal{D}$ for retrieval. When a user proposes a query $q$, the pipeline works as follows.

The rewriter model $M_W$ rewrites $q$ into a rewritten query set $Q^*$ which is better for retrieving relevant documents to answer $q$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:func1}
    Q^{*} = M_W(q).
\end{equation}
In some implementations, there is only one element in $Q^*$, i.e. it only generates one rewritten query for each user query.

Then, the retriever \(M_T\) retrieves a set of documents \(D\) based on  \(Q^{*}\):
\begin{equation}\label{eq:func2}
    D = M_T(Q^{*}).
\end{equation}

Finally, an LLM \(M_{R}\) produce the final answer \(a\) based on \(q, D\): 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:func3}
    a = M_{R}(q \circ D),
\end{equation}
where $\circ$ represents concatenation. 


\section{R\&R: Read the doc Before Rewriting}






We introduce our proposed R\&R in this section. We first introduce how R\&R rewrites a query during inference in the RAG pipeline in section \ref{sec: RR}. Then, we introduce the training process of R\&R in section \ref{sec: Train}. An overview of the R\&R is shown in Figure \ref{fig:methods_overview}. 



\subsection{Inference using R\&R} \label{sec: RR}
% The R\&R, is designed to refine the process depicted in Equation \ref{eq:func1}, contributes to a more pertinent document $D$ and more precise and accurate answer $ O $, as formulated in Equation  \ref{eq:func2} and \ref{eq:func3}.

% Suppose we have a rewrite model trained using our method, we introduce how to apply R\&R in the rewriter  in Equation \ref{eq:func1}



Our query rewriter is an LLM designed to identify knowledge points through in-context learning. The input to $M_W$ consists of an instruction, demonstration, and question as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:prompt_template}. 


Given $Q^*$, we retrieve relevant documents as follows. Each $q^*_i \in Q^*$ is transformed into an embedding vector $v_i$. Then, $v_i$ is compared against all document embedding vectors to determine their similarity and obtain the most similar \(k\) documents as a set $D(q^*_i)$. Finally, we select the top-\(k\) documents in $\bigcup_i D(q^*_i)$ among all rewritten queries. 


% This selection method aids in mitigating errors that may occur during the rewriting process, thereby enhancing the robustness of the retrieval process.



\subsection{Training R\&R} \label{sec: Train}

Our method focuses on improving rewriter $M_W$ using continual pertaining and finetuning.

\subsubsection{Continual Pretraining of R\&R}

We want to inject domain knowledge into the Rewriter to produce new queries that can bridge the lexical and knowledge gaps between the user query and the document.
So, we employ continual pretraining on the Rewriter with document data. The format of the document data used for pretraining is provided in the Appendix. 
