We now define a new language family. For each $i\geq 1$, let
\begin{equation}RPAL_i = \{s \mid s\in w\$\Sigma^* \cap RL_{i}, w\in PAL\}. 
\label[expression]{eqn:rpaldef} 
\end{equation} 

 The only difference between $RL_i$ and $RPAL_i$ is that Equation \ref{eqn:rpaldef} imposes the additional condition that the leftmost block of each member of the language should be a palindrome. Note that $RPAL_i$ can be seen as a ``padded'' version of the language $PAL \cap \Sigma_{\mathtt{ab}}\Sigma_{\mathtt{ab}}^+$, where the padding has a specific internal syntax. 
As we will demonstrate shortly,  a 2QCFA can check whether its input is in $RL_i$ in polynomial expected time  with a very low probability of error, and  the time complexity of recognizing $RPAL_i$ is dominated by the remaining necessity to check whether the leftmost block of the input is a palindrome or not.

