\begin{itemize}
    \item It is possible to obtain a 2QCFA procedure that operates on sequence pairs that span only part of the input tape. This is achieved by incorporating the  ability to recognize three ``signposts'' (denoted  $p_l$, $p_m$, and $p_r$ in the pseudocode in Figure \ref{fig:qalg1}) to the machine.  These are tape positions that delimit the two subsequences whose lengths are to be compared. The elements of the left (resp. right) subsequence are placed in the interval between  $p_l$ and  $p_m$ (resp. $p_m$ and $p_r$). () The precise triples of signposts  used as parameters by each submachine embodying the subsequence length comparison procedure \textsc{SameLength} in stages (C1) and (C2) are described in Figure \ref{fig:qalg1}. The reader should note that all described signposts can indeed be located by a finite-state machine, so that the input head will remain in the appropriate well-defined region and the machine will be ``aware'' of which subsequence it is traversing at all times during the execution of the procedure.     \item  $M_{EQ}$ can also be modified so that the individual elements in the subsequences whose lengths are being compared can be members of richer sets, including substrings of length more than one (such as, say, bin$(j)$  for some $j>1$). For instance, stage (C1) in Figure \ref{fig:qalg1} ``calls'' the \textsc{SameLength} procedure to compare the total number of occurrences of the elements of the set $\Sigma$ in the left interval with the number of occurrences of the substring bin$(j)$ in the right interval.\footnote{Note that the previously executed regular expression check in stage (R) guarantees that no two  bin$(j)$ substrings can overlap anywhere in the input during stage (C1).}  Essentially,  the modified machine ``runs'' the original  $M_{EQ}$ on a ``virtual tape'', where, for example, in stage (C1), a (multi-step) scan of the substring bin$(j)$ in the subsequence on the right will trigger the effects of a single $\mathtt{b}$-transition of  $M_{EQ}$ on its classical and quantum states. (The  identity ``transformation'' is applied on the quantum state during the additional steps taken by the modified machine to scan these long substrings.\footnote{Such multi-step scans may also be required when the head has to overshoot a symbol and check a symbol at an adjacent position in order to recognize a signpost. !!!!! HAYIR BU GEREKLİ DEĞİL!!!! For example, this is necessary for recognizing $p_m$ during the execution of stage (C1) when the head is moving from left to right on the tape.}) It easy to see that the 2QCFA can  ``skip'' over tape cells (without changing its quantum state) containing symbols other than those that are indicated as target members of the compared subsequences. 
\end{itemize}




