While visual inspection is a great tool and helps with basic understanding of similarity between clusters without the use of  any complex tools, a quantitative approach is need to identify the accuracy of clustering analysis and all four major available methods for datasets with accessible ground truth were used in this validation. The results for all four data cases are plotted in Figure \ref{fig:clustering_validation} and their respective numerical values expressed in Table \ref{table:clus_valid_table}. 
% Rand Index is between 0.16 to 0.21 with a range of 0.05, while Adjusted Rand Index varies between 0.63 to 0.65 with a range of 0.02. Mutual Information (MI) varies from 0.43 to 0.55 with a range of 0.12. Both forms of Mutual Information metrics – Normalized and Adjusted are very close and lie between 0.30 to 0.38 with a range of 0.08. Purity assessment metric of Homogeneity varies from 0.39 to 0.50 with a range of 0.11 and grouping assessment metric of Completeness varies from 0.24 to 0.31 with a range of 0.07. V-measure which balances purity and grouping varies from 0.30 to 0.38 with a range of 0.08. Finally, Fowlkes-Mallows Index varies from 0.40 to 0.44  with a range of 0.04.

While clustering parameters can be further optimized to improve these metrics, the focus of this framework is primarily on how these clustering metrics vary across our two dimensions -- with raw and SHAP value data, and with five vs eighteen sensors along with the implications of those dimensions on RUL bins for sensor constrained applications.
All cluster validation metrics show improved performance when using SHAP values instead of Raw data. When using all eighteen sensor inputs, the difference in metrics for using SHAP values improves by a maximum of 0.03 for Mutual Index scores, Homogeneity, and V-measure.
This effect is less profound in sensor constrained applications with five sensors where a maximum difference of 0.01 is observed for SHAP values clusters in Mutual Index and Homogeneity metrics. All other metrics remain constant until the second significant digit. This implies that for sensor constrained data cases, we can simply use the maintenance bins to identify scheduling times from five sensors of raw data clusters instead of calculating clusters using SHAP values. Further work needs to be carried out in this regard to explore the number of sensors with an aim to obtain further reduction in sensor numbers. 


