\subsubsection{Requests and Links} We consider a discrete-time system where, at the beginning of each time slot, requests of $R$ types arrive. We denote $\mathcal{R}$ as the set of $R$ request types and $\mathcal{L}$ as the set of $L$ links. The switch stores requests of each type in their corresponding infinite-capacity queue and processes them on a First-Come-First-Served (FCFS) basis. 
A type $r\in \mathcal{R}$ request is considered served when the switch establishes an $n_r$-qubit end-to-end entanglement among a set of links denoted by $\mathcal L_r = \{l_{r,1}, \dots, l_{r,n_r}\} \subset \mathcal{L}$.
