\section{Technical Overview}
\label{sec:warm-up}
We sketch the proof of the following theorem while outlining the ideas used in our main results. 

\begin{theorem}
\label{th:warm-up}
    Let $\mathcal{S}$ be the set of finite templates $(\A, \B)$ with $|A|\leq 3$ such that $\pcsp(\A, \B)$ is solved by $\AIP$. Then (1) the family of problems $\spcsp_{\AIP}(\A, \B)$ where $(\A, \B)\in \mathcal{S}$ is TFNP$_1$-hard, (2) $\mathcal{S}$ is undecidable, and (3) there is a template
    $(\A, \B)\in \mathcal{S}$ for which there is no computable homomorphism $F: \Mscr_\AIP \rightarrow \pol(\A, \B)$.
\end{theorem}

The proof of this result is a reduction from tiling problems. The signature $\Sigma_\Gamma$  consists of a unary symbol $O$ and binary symbols $E_1,E_2$, and $\bm \Gamma$ is the $\Sigma_\Gamma$-structure whose universe is the upper-right quadrant $\NN^2$, and where $O^\Gamma=\{ (1,1) \}$ marks the origin, and $E_1^\Gamma=\{ ((m,n),(m+1,n)) \, \vert \, (m,n)\in \NN^2 \}$,  $E_2^\Gamma=\{ ((m,n),(m,n+1)) \, \vert \, (m,n)\in \NN^2 \}$ 
are the horizontal and vertical adjacency relations. We write $\Hom(\bm \Gamma, \cdot)$ for the set of finite structures $\bT$ satisfying $\bm \Gamma \rightarrow \bT$.




\begin{restatable}{proposition}{gridprop}
\label{prop:grid}
The following hold: (1)
the family of problems $\spcsp(\bm \Gamma, \bT)$ where $\bT\in \Hom(\bm \Gamma, \cdot)$ is TFNP$_1$-hard, (2) $\Hom(\bm \Gamma, \cdot)$ is undecidable, and (3) there exists $\bT\in \Hom(\bm \Gamma, \cdot)$ for which there is no computable homomorphism $F: \bm \Gamma \rightarrow \bT$.
\end{restatable}


At a high-level, this is follows from the fact that, given a non-deterministic Turing machine $M$, we can construct a finite structure $\bT_M$ for which $F:\bm \Gamma \rightarrow \bT_M$ represent non-halting runs of $\bT_M$. \par
In order to prove \Cref{th:warm-up} using this proposition we first place $\bm \Gamma$ inside the minion $\Mscr_\AIP$, and then we find a way of encoding finite $\Sigma_\Gamma$-structures in finite templates $(\A, \B)$ in a suitable way. The first task is the simpler one. We can identify each pair $\bm m= (m_1,m_2)$ in $\NN^2$ with the $3$-ary element $ f_{\bm m} = (m_1,m_2, 1-m_1 -m_2)\in \Mscr_\AIP$. Similarly, we can represent each pair $(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)\in E_i^\Gamma$ with the $4$-ary element $g_{E_i(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)}=(m_1,m_2,1, - m_1 - m_2)$ with the idea that $f_{\bm m}= g_{E_i(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)}^{(1,2,3,3)}$ and 
$f_{\bm m^\prime}= g_{E_i(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)}^{(1,2,i,3)}$ in mind. We call this way of representing a relational structure inside a minion an \emph{interpretation} (\Cref{sec:interpretations}) and we denote it by $\Ical$. We define $U^\Ical= \{ f_{\bm m} \, \vert \, \bm m\in \NN^2\}$, $O^\Ical=\{ f_{(1,1)} \}$, and $E_i^\Ical = \{ g_{E_i(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)} \, \vert \, (\bm m, \bm m^\prime)\in E_i^\Gamma \}$ for $i=1,2$.
\par

The interpretation $\Ical$ is \emph{almost} pp-definable in $\Mscr_\AIP$. Indeed, the element $(1,0)\in \Mscr_\AIP(2)$ is the only witness of the pp-formula $\phi_1(x^2)\equiv x=x^{(1,1)}$, so the $4$-ary elements of the form $(m_1,m_2,1, - m_1 - m_2)\in \Mscr_\AIP$ are precisely the witnesses of $\phi_E(y)\equiv \phi_1(y^{(2,2,1,2)})$. However, this does not take into account the restriction that $m_1, m_2\in \NN$. Under closer inspection it is not hard to see that the set of elements $(m,n)\in \Mscr_\AIP$ with $m\in \NN$ is not pp-definable, so, in fact, the interpretation $\Ical$ is not pp-definable \footnote{This could be circumvented by having defined $\bm \Gamma$ on the whole integer plane $\ZZ^2$ instead of $\NN^2$, but we want to illustrate the point that our reductions do not result from pp-definitions.}. However, we get something almost as good: for any element $\bm m\in \NN^2$ we can construct in polynomial time a pp-definition $\psi_{\bm m}(x)$ of $f_{\bm m}$ in $\Mscr_\AIP$. For $\bm m=(1,1)$, we simply define $\psi_{\bm m}(x)\equiv \phi_1(x^{(1,2,2)}) \wedge \phi_1(x^{(2,1,2)})$. 
Given $\bm m\in \NN^2$, for $\bm m^\prime=(m_1+1,m_2)$, we let
\[
\psi_{\bm m}(x)\equiv \bigexists^{4} y \bigexists^3 z \left(\psi_{\bm m}(z) \wedge \phi_{E}(y) \wedge z = y^{(1,2,3,3)} \wedge
x = y^{(1,2,1,3)}\right),
\]
and so on. This way, given a finite substructure $\bm G \subset \bm \Gamma$, we can construct in polynomial time a minor condition $\psi_{\bm G}$ with existentially quantified variables $\{ x_{\bm m}\}_{\bm m\in G} \cup \{ y_{E_i(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)}\}_{i\in [2],(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)\in E_i}$ whose satisfying assignments on $\Mscr_\AIP$ must map $x_{\bm m}\mapsto f_{\bm m}$ and
$y_{E_i(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)} \mapsto g_{E_i(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)}$. Indeed, define
\begin{multline}
\label{eq:pattern_warm-up}
 \Psi_{\bm G} \equiv 
\bigexists_{\bm m \in G}^3 x_{\bm m}
\bigexists_{i\in [2], (\bm m, \bm m^\prime)\in E_i^G}^4
y_{E_i(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)} 
\left( \bigwedge_{\bm m\in G}
\psi_{\bm m}(x_{\bm m})
\right)  \bigwedge \\
 \left( \bigwedge_{i\in [2], (\bm m, \bm m^\prime)\in E_i^G}
\psi_{E}(y_{E_i(\bm m,\bm m^\prime)}) \wedge  x_{\bm m}= y_{E_i(\bm m,\bm m^\prime)}^{(1,2,3,3)} \wedge  x_{\bm m^\prime}= y_{E_i(\bm m,\bm m^\prime)}^{(1,2,i,3)} 
\right)
\end{multline}
This construction is called a \emph{pattern} (\Cref{sec:patterns}). Having found a nice way to represent $\bm \Gamma$ inside $\Mscr_\AIP$, the next step is to develop a construction that, given a finite $\Sigma_\Gamma$-structure $\bT$, finds a suitable finite template $(\A_{\bT}, \B_{\bT})$. We would like $\Mscr_{\bT}= \pol(\A_{\bT}, \B_{\bT})$ to satisfy the following properties: 
    \[
    \label{eq:warm-up_conditions}
    \tag{$\star$}\parbox{0.9\textwidth}{
    \begin{enumerate}[label=({\arabic*})]
        \item $\Mscr_\AIP\rightarrow \Mscr_{\bT}$ if and only if $\bm \Gamma \rightarrow \bT$.
        \item A homomorphism $F: \bm \Gamma \rightarrow \bT$ can be computed given oracle access to a homomorphism $H: \Mscr_\AIP\rightarrow \Mscr_{\bT}$.
        \item Given a finite substructure $\bm G \subset \bm \Gamma$, there is a polynomial-time reduction from the task of finding a homomorphism $F: \bm G \rightarrow \bT$ to the task of finding a satisfying assignment of $\psi_{\bm G}$ in $\Mscr_{\bT}$.
    \end{enumerate}
    }\]
  It is easy enough to see that properties (1) and (2) allow us to respectively reduce the undecidability and non-computability parts of \Cref{th:warm-up} to those of \Cref{prop:grid}. In to bridge the gap between the TFNP$_1$-hardness statements in those results, we use property (3) together with the following additional result, which is a consequence of \Cref{th:spcsp_to_spmc} (\Cref{sec:reductions}). 
\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:PMC_reduction_warm-up}
    Suppose that $\AIP$ solves $\pcsp(\A, \B)$ for a finite template $(\A, \B)$. Then $\spcsp_\AIP(\A,\B)$ is log-space equivalent to $\sPMC_N(\Mscr_\AIP, \pol(\A, \B))$, where $N$ is at least as large as $|A|$ and $|R^A|$ for all relation symbols $R$.
\end{proposition}

Rather than constructing the template $(\A_{\bT}, \B_{\bT})$ directly, we focus on its polymorphism minion instead.
We start by defining a minion $\Nscr_{\bT}$ that has all desired properties \eqref{eq:warm-up_conditions} of $\pol(\A_{\bT}, \B_{\bT})$. The $n$-ary elements of $\Nscr_{\bT}$ are pairs $(f,\chi)$, where $f\in \Mscr_{\AIP}(n)$, and $\chi: [3]^{[n]}\partialmap T$ is a partial map
defined on the elements $\gamma\in [3]^{[n]}$ such that $f^\gamma\in U^\Ical$ that satisfies the following properties:  if $f_{(1,1)}=f^\gamma$, then $\chi(\gamma)\in O^T$, and if $g_{E_i(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)}=f^\pi$, then 
$(\chi(\gamma), \chi(\gamma^\prime))\in E_i^T$, where $\gamma=(1,2,3,3)\circ \pi$, and $\gamma^\prime=(1,2,i,3)\circ \pi$. Intuitively, the element $f\in \Mscr_{\AIP}$ \emph{covers} some part of $\bm \Gamma$ (as interpreted by $\Ical$), which consists of the elements $f_{\bm m}\in U^\Ical$ that can be obtained by minoring $f$. Similarly, we also think of $f$ as covering the edges $(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)\in E_i^\Gamma$ where $g_{E_i(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)}\in E_i^\Ical$ is a minor of $f$. Then, the map $\chi$ represents a homomorphism from the substructure $\bLS_f\subseteq \bm \Gamma$ covered by $f$ to $\bT$. Given a map $\pi\in [m]^{[n]}$, we define the minor $(f,\chi)^{\pi}$ as the pair $(f^\pi, \chi^\pi)$, where $\chi^\pi: [3]^{[m]} \partialmap T$ is the partial map defined by $\gamma \mapsto \chi(\gamma \circ \pi)$. The rationale behind this construction is that the structure $\bLS_{f^\pi}$ covered by $f^\pi$ is a substructure of $\bLS_{f}$, so $\chi^\pi$ is defined so that it represents the restriction of $\chi$ to $\bLS_{f^\pi}$.
If the substructures $\bLS_f$ formed a topology over $\bm \Gamma$ (which, we remark, is not the case), then the minion $\Nscr_{\bT}$ would encode a \emph{sheaf} \cite{bredon2012sheaf} over $\bm \Gamma$ whose \emph{sections} correspond to partial homomorphisms to $\bT$. We keep this topological intuition in mind and refer to the maps $\chi$ in $(f,\chi)$ as sections. We call $\Nscr_{\bT}$ the \emph{exponential minion} given by the interpretation $\Ical$ and the structure $\bT$ (\Cref{sec:exponential_minion}). \par
\begin{fact}
    The minion $\Nscr_{\bT}$ satisfies \eqref{eq:warm-up_conditions}.
\end{fact}
Let us sketch the proof of this fact. It is not difficult to show that every element $f\in \Mscr_\AIP$ is pp-definable, so the only minion homomorphism $F: \Mscr_\AIP \rightarrow \Mscr_\AIP$ is the identity.  Hence, any homomorphism $F:\Mscr_\AIP \rightarrow \Nscr_{\bT}$ composed with the left projection must yield the identity over $\Mscr_\AIP$. In other words, $F$ must be of the form $f \mapsto (f,\chi_f)$. If such homomorphism $F$ exists, then the sections $\chi_f$ must be compatible with each other, yielding a global homomorphism from $\bm \Gamma$ to $\bT$. Conversely, if there is a homomorphism $H:\bm \Gamma \rightarrow \bT$, one can restrict it to each local structure $\bLS_f$ to obtain compatible sections $\chi_f$ and define a minion homomorphism $F$. This proves $\Nscr_{\bT}$ satisfies (1). To show property (2), suppose that we have oracle access to a homomorphism $F:\Mscr_\AIP \rightarrow \Nscr_{\bT}$. Then, by the previous reasoning, in order to compute a homomorphism $H:\bm \Gamma \rightarrow \bT$, given an element $\bm m\in \NN^2$ we just need to query $F(f_{\bm m})=(f_{\bm m}, \chi_{f_{\bm m}})$ and consider the section $\chi_{f_{\bm m}}$. Finally, consider a finite substructure $\bG \subset \bm \Gamma$, and a satisfying assignment $x\mapsto (f_x,\chi_x)$ of $\Psi_{\bG}$ in $\Nscr_{\bT}$. The map $x\mapsto f_x$
is a satisfying assignment of $\Psi_{\bG}$ in $\Mscr_\AIP$, so by construction of $\Psi_{\bG}$ it must hold that $f_{x}=f_{\bm m}$ when $x=x_{\bm m}$ for each $\bm m\in G$, and $f_{x}=g_{E_i(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)}$ when $x= y_{E_i(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)}$ for each $i\in [2]$, $(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)\in E_i^G$. Hence, when $x$ ranges over all variables $\{ x_{\bm m}\}_{\bm m\in G} \cup \{ y_{E_i(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)}\}_{i\in [2],(\bm m, \bm m^\prime)\in E_i}$, the elements $f_x$ cover the whole structure $\bG$ (as induced by the interpretation $\Ical$), and the charts $\chi_x$ must 
piece together a homomorphism from $\bG$ to $\bT$. This shows that $\Nscr_{\bT}$ satisfies property (3). \par
The main issue at this point is that, despite having the desired properties, the minion $\Nscr_{\bT}$ is not (isomorphic to) the polymorphism minion of any finite template $(\A_{\bT}, \B_{\bT})$. In fact, it is not even locally finite. The first step towards constructing $(\A_{\bT}, \B_{\bT})$ from the minion $\Nscr_{\bT}$ is to find a locally finite quotient $\Nscr^\prime_{\bT}$ of $\Nscr_{\bT}$ that still satisfies \eqref{eq:warm-up_conditions}. More precisely, $\Nscr^\prime_{\bT}$ will be obtained by performing the exponential construction on a locally finite quotient of $\Mscr_\AIP$ by some equivalence relation $\sim$. This relation also induces an equivalence on $\bm \Gamma$ by means of the interpretation $\Ical$, which we denote $\sim$ as well, defined as $\bm m \sim \bm m^\prime$ whenever $f_{\bm m}\sim f_{\bm m^\prime}$. We write $\qclass{f}$ for the $\sim$-class of an element $f\in \Mscr_\AIP$, and, similarly, $\qclass{m}$ for the $\sim$-class of $\bm m\in \NN^2$.
We would like the relation $\sim$ to satisfy the following:
    \[
    \label{eq:warm-up_conditions2}
    \tag{$\star\star$}\parbox{0.9\textwidth}{
    \begin{enumerate}[label=({\arabic*})]
        \item For each partial homomorphism $F: \Mscr_\AIP \partialmap \Mscr_\AIP/\sim$ defined up to arity $4$, it holds that $F(f_{(1,1)})=\qclass{f_{(1,1)}}$,
        $F(U^\Ical)\subseteq U^\Ical/\sim$, 
        and
        $F(E_i^\Ical)\subseteq E_i^\Ical/\sim$ for each $i\in [2]$.
        \item Given a finite substructure $\bm G \subset \bm \Gamma$, any satisfying assignment of $\Psi_{\bm G}$ in $\Mscr_{\AIP}/\sim$ must correspond to a homomorphism from $\bm G$ into $\Gamma/\sim$.
    \end{enumerate}
    }\]
Observe that (1) would be weaker if we considered non-partial homomorphisms instead. We consider partial homomorphisms planning for a future step where, in order to obtain a polymorphism minion, we will have to let go of high-arity terms in a certain way. The choice of arity $4$ corresponds
to the fact that the minor conditions $\Psi_{\bm G}$ have arity bounded by $4$. 
Roughly, condition (2) requires that any assignment of $\Psi_{\bm G}$ in $\Mscr_{\AIP}/\sim$ maps, for each $\bm m\in G$, the variable $x_{\bm m}$ to some element $\qclass{f_{\bm m^\prime}}$ in such a way that the map $\bm m \mapsto \qclass{\bm m^\prime}$ is
a homomorphism from $\bG$ to $\bm \Gamma/\sim$. \par
In order to construct $\sim$ we select some relevant \emph{properties} (related to $\Ical$) containing the information that we would like to preserve in the quotient. These properties will be sets of binary elements $D_1, D_{\NN}\subseteq \Mscr_\AIP(2)$ defined as $D_1=\{(1,0)\}$, and $D_\NN=\{(m,1-m) \, \vert \, m\in \NN \}$. We call the set of properties $\Dcal=\{ D_1, D_\NN\}$ a \emph{description} (\Cref{sec:descriptions}). Then, we write $f\sim_\Dcal g$ for two elements $f, g\in \Mscr_\AIP$ of the same arity $n$ whenever for all $\pi\in [2]^{[n]}$ and all $P\in \Dcal$ the inclusion $f^\pi\in P$ holds if and only if $g^\pi\in P$ as well. \Cref{fig:quotient_warm-up}
displays the quotient $\bm \Gamma/\Dcal$.
\begin{fact}
    The equivalence relation $\sim_\Dcal$ satisfies \eqref{eq:warm-up_conditions2}.
\end{fact}
This is, roughly, a consequence of the fact that the formula
$\phi_{\bm m}(x)$ is a pp-definition of $\qclass{f_{\bm m}}$ in $\Mscr_\AIP/\Dcal$ for each $\bm m\in\NN^2$. Let us sketch this fact. First, we can see that $\phi_1(x^2)\equiv x= x^{(1,1)}$ defines the element $\qclass{(1,0)}$. Indeed,
if $\Mscr_\AIP/\Dcal \models \phi_1(\qclass{f})$ then $f\sim_\Dcal f^{(1,1)}$. However, $f^{(1,1)}\in D_1$ for all $f\in \Mscr_\AIP(2)$, so $f$ must belong to $D_1$ as well, meaning that $f=(1,0)$. Following this argument, 
we see that $\phi_{(1,1)}(x)$ defines $\qclass{f_{(1,1)}}$, and the other values of $\bm m\in \NN^2$ can be handled by induction. \par 
The equivalence $\sim_\Dcal$ induces a quotient interpretation $\Jcal=\Ical/\Dcal$ on $\Mscr_\AIP/\Dcal$: the same way that $\Ical$ interprets $\bm{\Gamma}$ over $\Mscr_\AIP$, $\Jcal$ interprets $\bm \Gamma/\Dcal$ over
$\Mscr/\Dcal$. Then we define $\Nscr^\prime_{\bT}$ as the exponential minion given by the interpretation $\Jcal$ and
the structure $\bT$. The fact that $\sim_\Dcal$ satisfies \eqref{eq:warm-up_conditions2}, means that $\Nscr^\prime_{\bT}$
manages to preserve the properties \eqref{eq:warm-up_conditions} that $\Nscr_{\bT}$ satisfied. However, we are still not done: The minion $\Nscr^\prime_{\bT}$ is locally finite, but it is still not isomorphic to a polymorphism minion. The reason is that it is not finitizable in the sense of \cite{BG21:sicomp}. To overcome this, we prove the following.

\begin{proposition}
    There is a finite template $(\A_{\bT}, \B_{\bT})$ with $|A|=3$ and $|R^A|\leq 4$ for any relation symbol $R$ satisfying that there is a partial minion isomorphism $F:\Nscr^\prime_{\bT} \partialmap \pol(\A, \B)$ defined up to arity $4$.
\end{proposition}

This is a consequence of the more general result \Cref{th:minion_to_template} (\Cref{sec:minion_closures}). The template $(\A, \B)$ is constructed as follows. We consider a single relation symbol $R$ of arity $3^4$. Then $\A$ is \emph{the most general structure} on $3$ elements containing a single relation $R^A$ of size $4$, following a construction given in \cite{BG21:sicomp}. The fact that $|A|=3$ in this result has to do with the fact that any two elements $f,g\in \Nscr^\prime_{\bT}$ of the same arity are equal if and only if all their $3$-ary minors are equal. Hence, $\Nscr^\prime_{\bT}$ can be seen as a function minion on a domain of size $3$. The structure $\B$ is defined as the \emph{free-structure} \cite{BBKO21} of $\Nscr^\prime_{\bT}$ generated by $\A$. The fact that we do not obtain a full isomorphism in this proposition may seem concerning, but we already accounted for this by
considering partial homomorphisms in \eqref{eq:warm-up_conditions2}. Hence, the following completes the proof of \Cref{th:warm-up}.

\begin{fact}
    Let $(\A_{\bT}, \B_{\bT})$ be the template obtained in last proposition. Then $\pol(\A_{\bT}, \B_{\bT})$ satisfies \eqref{eq:warm-up_conditions}.
\end{fact}



