\begin{abstract}
    We present the first unsupervised learning model for finding Maximum Independent Sets (MaxIS) in dynamic graphs where edges change over time.
    Our method combines structural learning from graph neural networks (GNNs) with a learned distributed update mechanism that, given an edge addition or deletion event, modifies nodes' internal memories and infers their MaxIS membership in a single, parallel step.
    We parameterize our model by the update mechanism's radius and investigate the resulting performance--runtime tradeoffs for various dynamic graph topologies.
    We evaluate our model against state-of-the-art MaxIS methods for static graphs, including a mixed integer programming solver, deterministic rule-based algorithms, and a heuristic learning framework based on dynamic programming and GNNs.
    Across synthetic and real-world dynamic graphs of 100--\numprint{10000} nodes, our model achieves competitive approximation ratios with excellent scalability; on large graphs, it significantly outperforms the state-of-the-art heuristic learning framework in solution quality, runtime, and memory usage.
    Our model generalizes well on graphs \numprint{100}x larger than the ones used for training, achieving performance at par with both a greedy technique and a commercial mixed integer programming solver while running 1.5--23x faster than greedy.
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction} \label{sec:intro}

Combinatorial optimization problems on graphs (e.g., \textsc{Graph-Coloring}, \textsc{Traveling-Salesman}, \textsc{Vehicle-Routing}, etc.) arise naturally in many practical domains \citep{applegate2007traveling,toth2014vehicle,hochbaum1997approximation}.
However, many of these problems are classically intractable to solve exactly, and some even resist efficient approximation.
The situation is exacerbated by the fact that many real-world applications are \textit{dynamic}, requiring algorithms to not only find solutions but also maintain them as the underlying structure evolves.
While one could iteratively apply algorithms designed for static graphs at each time step, this approach is often computationally expensive and inefficient.
Critically, this approach ignores the often close relationship between successive graph structures.
A more effective strategy might avoid repeatedly recomputing solutions from scratch by instead leveraging these small structural changes to incrementally update an existing solution.
Such \textit{update algorithms} are an active area of theoretical algorithms research \citep{Hanauer_Henzinger_Schulz_2022,Chechik_Zhang_2021,behnezhad_fully_2019}, but no learning algorithms have yet taken this approach.

In this paper, we consider the \textsc{Maximum-Independent-Set} (MaxIS) problem in a dynamic setting.
% This problem is closely related to other combinatorial optimization problems, such as \textsc{Maximum-Clique} and \textsc{Minimum-Vertex-Cover}.
For static graphs, MaxIS is classically known to be NP-hard \citep{Garey1979-computersintractability} and hard to approximate: for general graphs, deterministic approximation within a constant factor is impossible \citep{Robson1986-algorithmsmaximum} and within an $n^{1-\varepsilon}$ factor is NP-hard \citep{Hastad1996-cliquehard}.
Nevertheless, MaxIS has a diverse range of real-world applications, from identifying functional nodes in brain networks \citep{Afek_Alon_Barad_Hornstein_Barkai_Bar-Joseph_2011} to constructing diversified investment portfolios \citep{hidaka_yohei_hamakawa_nakayama_kosuketatsumura_2023} to scheduling in public transportation systems \citep{gainanov_damir_n_mladenovic_nenad_a_2018}.
Each use case can be modeled as a conflict graph, where an edge represents a conflict between selecting two nodes.
For example, in investment portfolio construction, diversification is critical; if one stock declines, others in the portfolio should not be closely related.
In this case, the nodes represent investments, and the edges represent dependencies or correlations between them.
Similarly, in transportation scheduling, conflicts may arise due to overlapping routes or timing constraints.
In all these situations, the conflict graph changes dynamically due to factors such as stock market fluctuations, train delays, and other unpredictable events.
Our goal is to solve MaxIS on such dynamically evolving graphs.

Recently, the learning community has developed several methods for solving combinatorial optimization problems on static graphs \citep{Bengio_Lodi_Prouvost_2021}.
Central to many of these methods are Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) due to their efficiency in capturing the structural information present in the real-world networks such as the Internet, social networks, and molecular interactions \citep{wu2021comprehensive,Zhang_Tong_Xu_Maciejewski_2019}.
In the dynamic setting, recent models such as temporal GNNs are primarily concerned with tasks such as edge prediction, node classification, graph classification, etc.\ \citep{Rossi2020,Xu_inductive_2019}.
Work considering combinatorial optimization problems in a dynamic setting remains limited, with few examples such as \citet{Gunarathna_Borovica-Gajic_Karunasekera_Tanin_2022}, which is an indirect heuristic learning approach.
However, no models exist to learn update mechanisms comparable to theoretical, rule-based update algorithms.

We propose a model that solves MaxIS in dynamic graphs by directly learning an update mechanism instead of a heuristic learning.
We utilize the power of message-passing GNNs \citep{Battaglia_Hamrick_Bapst_Sanchez_2018} combined with sequence learning to learn an update mechanism that maintains an approximate MaxIS solution as the underlying graph changes.
GNNs learn to aggregate structural information and update a node's membership in the MaxIS solution at any instance of a dynamic graph, while sequence learning learns the effect of a change in the dynamic graph on the change in the MaxIS solution.
Having learned an update mechanism, the model is able to update a MaxIS solution following a change in the dynamic graph in a single inference step, unlike more popular heuristic learning methods which require multiple inference steps and thus scale poorly with graph size.
We underscore this as a necessary shift in perspective required to address some of the common criticisms of learning approaches to solving combinatorial optimization problems, namely, lack of scalability, computation inefficiency, and huge training sample requirements. 


\paragraph{Contributions.}

We present the first unsupervised learning model for solving the \textsc{Maximum-Independent-Set} (MaxIS) problem on dynamic graphs.
% , while maintaining competitive approximation ratios.
Compared to a commercial mixed integer programming solver \citep{gurobi}, our model maintains competitive approximation ratios across a variety of dynamic graph topologies, sizes, and edge dynamics.
For larger datasets, the model conclusively outperforms DP-GNN \citep{Brusca2023}, the state-of-the-art GNN-based model for MaxIS in static graphs.
Resource scaling for our method vs.\ DP-GNN is drastic; we obtain solutions up to $10^4$ times faster and use up to \numprint{27.5}x less memory.
Our model is also the first learning method to successfully learn an update mechanism for the dynamic version of any NP-hard combinatorial optimization problem.




\section{Related Work} \label{sec:relwork}

\paragraph*{Traditional Algorithms for MaxIS.}

Maximum Independent Set (MaxIS), an NP-complete problem, has been a long-standing problem in graph theory and has been explored since the 1970s in the field of combinatorial optimization. It has been studied extensively in the context of static graphs. There have been a number of attempts to achieve an exact algorithm for the problem using reduction rules \citep{Tarjan1977-findingmaximum}, branching heuristics \citep{Fomin_Kaski_2013}, and kernelization techniques \citep{lamm2017finding} that compress the graph size being worked on. \citet{Xiao2017} have an algorithm that promises a run time of $1.1996^nn^{O(1)}$ and polynomial space where $n$ is the number of vertices in the graph.

While a wide variety of work exists with respect to static graphs, the dynamic graph instances relatively remain unexplored. Using the rule-based optimization techniques, the dynamic network version of the problem has been explored in the past 5 years. \citet{Zheng2019} and \citet{Gao2022} explore how these techniques can be exploited to maintain a MaxIS for a dynamically evolving network. These approaches typically rely on maintaining and updating an existing solution incrementally in response to edge insertions or deletions.


\paragraph*{Learning in Dynamic Graphs.}

We primarily look at the dynamic graph models called memory-based models due to their efficiency and success in learning prediction and classification tasks in dynamic graphs \citep{Rossi2020, Kumar_Zhang_Leskovec_2019,Trivedi_Farajtabar_Biswal_Zha_2018,yu_towards_2023}. They utilize some form of persistent high-dimensional representation (i.e., a memory) for nodes, which is used to learn the dynamics of the graph as it changes. Some of them also include a GNN-based graph embedding module, which helps in mitigating the problem of memory staleness, i.e., when a node's memory hasn't been updated for a long time. We utilize these innovations in our model. Other models that do not involve memory \citep{Xu_inductive_2019, Pareja_EvolveGCN_2020} exist and are covered in a recent survey paper \citep{Longa_Graph_2023} on the latest models for learning in dynamic graphs. We highlight that these models do not consider combinatorial optimization problems.  


\paragraph*{Learning for Combinatorial Optimization.}

A vast body of machine learning research into solving combinatorial optimization problems exists \citep{Peng_Choi_Xu_2021, Khalil_Dai_Zhang_Dilkina_Song_2017, Bengio_Lodi_Prouvost_2021}. And, GNNs are the most common backbone for much of this work given their excellent ability to characterize the structural information of graphs \citep{wu2021comprehensive,Zhang_Tong_Xu_Maciejewski_2019}. MaxIS is one such problem that has garnered attention from the learning community, and autoregressive approaches (take multiple inference steps to output a solution), such as RL and Dynamic Programming have emerged as a natural choice due to their easy constraint enforcement and a priori integral solution output \citep{Brusca2023,Ahn_Seo_Shin_2020}. They act as indirect heuristic learning methods. Yet, these methods suffer from sample inefficiency and do not scale well (number of inference steps required scales linearly with problem size) \citep{Peng_Choi_Xu_2021, Karalias_Loukas_2020}. Non-autoregressive approaches, i.e., single-shot methods (output a solution in one inference step), have been proposed as well \citep{Karalias_Loukas_2020, Li_combinatorial_2018,Wang_Wu_Yang_Hao_Li_2022,Schuetz_Brubaker_Katzgraber_2022} with unsupervised learning being a key attribute of these techniques to mitigate the issue of labeling a vast number of solutions. However, these techniques only investigate static graphs. The only other machine learning model (to the best of our knowledge) that considers combinatorial optimization problems in dynamic graphs is by \citet{Gunarathna_Borovica-Gajic_Karunasekera_Tanin_2022}, and is an RL technique for solving the Traveling Salesman Problem and Vehicle Routing Problem. The most recent and state-of-the-art method for solving MaxIS in static graphs is by \citet{Brusca2023}, which is a dynamic programming-based method and suffers from aforementioned drawbacks of autoregressive approaches. Our work is the first single-shot learning method for a combinatorial optimization problem in dynamic graphs.




\section{Maximum Independent Sets in Dynamic Graphs} \label{sec:prelim}

Consider an undirected, static graph $G = (V, E)$ with nodes $V$ and edges $E$.
An \textit{independent set} $I \subseteq V$ of $G$ is a set of nodes no two of which are adjacent, i.e., for all $u, v \in I$, we have $(u, v) \not\in E$.
Its \textit{size} is its number of nodes, denoted $|I|$.
A \textit{maximum independent set} (MaxIS) of $G$ is any independent set $I$ of $G$ satisfying $|I| = \max\{|I'| : I' \text{ is an independent set of } G\}$.

In general, \textit{dynamic graphs} (also called dynamic networks, temporal graphs, or evolving graphs) are graphs whose nodes and/or edges change over time~\citep{Casteigts_Flocchini_Quattrociocchi_Santoro_2012, Rossi2020, Kazemi_Goel_Jain_Kobyzev_Sethi_Forsyth_Poupart_2020,Bui-Xuan2003-computingshortest}.
For this work, we consider dynamic graphs where at most one edge changes per time and the underlying set of nodes is fixed.
Formally, a dynamic graph $\mathcal{G}$ is a finite sequence of \textit{graph snapshots} $(G_0, G_1, \ldots, G_T)$ where each snapshot $G_t = (V, E_t)$ is an undirected, static graph on nodes $V$ and, for all time steps $0 < t \leq T$, we have $|E_{t-1} \oplus E_t| = 1$, i.e., there is exactly one edge addition or one edge deletion per time step.
We denote the \textit{edge event} at time $t$ which transitions $G_{t-1}$ to $G_t$ as $\mathcal{E}_t$. The \textit{neighborhood} of a node $v$ at time $t$ is $\mathcal{N}_t(v) = \{u \in V \mid (u,v) \in  E_t\}$ and its \textit{degree} is denoted $d_t(v) = |\mathcal{N}_t(v)|$.
The diameter of graph snapshot $G_t$, denoted $\text{diam}(G_t)$, is the maximum hop-distance between any two nodes in $G_t$.

We define the \ourproblem\ (Dynamic MaxIS) problem as follows:
Given a dynamic graph $\mathcal{G} = (G_0, G_1, \ldots, G_T)$---or, equivalently, an initial graph snapshot $G_0$ and a sequence of edge events $(\mathcal{E}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{E}_T)$---obtain a MaxIS for each snapshot $G_t$.
This problem is clearly NP-hard since it contains the NP-complete MaxIS problem as a special case ($T = 0$).
Given a solution for the MaxIS problem, there exist a polynomial-time conversions to obtain solutions for the \textsc{Minimum-Vertex-Cover} and \textsc{Maximum-Clique} problems; hence, solving Dynamic MaxIS can also aid in solving these problems' dynamic versions.




\section{An Unsupervised Learning Model for Dynamic MaxIS} \label{sec:model}

% \begin{figure}[t]
%     \centering
%     \includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{Figures/GNNFramework.png}
%     \caption{Temporary model figure to capture a single step of the model}
%     \label{fig:framework}
% \end{figure}

As discussed in Section \ref{sec:relwork}, memory-based models have been quite successful in recent years.
Motivated by their effectiveness in predictive learning of dynamics, we present an unsupervised learning model focused on the MaxIS problem in dynamic graphs.
We take inspiration from the Temporal Graph Networks (TGN) model proposed by \citet{Rossi2020}, which generalizes memory-based models by including a GNN-based graph embedding module.
We repurpose the two key modules of TGNs, memory and graph embedding, to learn an update mechanism that maintains a candidate MaxIS solution as the underlying graph changes.
Moreover, inspired by the use of messages (seen as random perturbations) to improve GNN performance for NP-hard problems \citep{Papp_dropgnn_2021}, we couple node memory to an event handling module to inform nodes about edge events.

Our model responds to edge events (i.e., edge additions or deletions) over time.
At each time step, the \textit{event handling module} is responsible for signaling all nodes within an $\alpha$ hop-distance of the edge event that the event occurred.
These nodes then use the \textit{memory module} to update their internal representations using this signal.
Finally, each node within a $\beta$ hop-distance of the edge event uses the \textit{local aggregation (graph embedding) module} to produce a probability of membership in the MaxIS solution (i.e., an \textit{estimate}) based on its memory, its degree, and the memories of its neighbors.
We describe each module and the training-related details of our model in the following subsections.


\paragraph*{Event Handling Module.} \label{subsec:msggen}

This module provides each node $v$ within an $\alpha$ hop-distance of the edge event $\mathcal{E}_t$ with a signal $s_t(v)$ encoding the type of event (addition or deletion) and the node's normalized distance from the event.
Formally, the signal for node $v$ at time $t$ is
\[s_t(v) = [\texttt{enc}(\mathcal{E}_t)~||~r_t(v)],\]
where \texttt{enc} encodes edge deletions as $[0, 1]$ and edge additions as $[1, 0]$, and $r_t(v)$ is the hop-distance from $v$ to $\mathcal{E}_t$ normalized by $\alpha$ and linearly interpolated into $[-1, 1]$.


\paragraph*{Memory Module.}

A node's memory is a high-dimensional representation maintained throughout the model's execution that captures relevant structural changes affecting the node.
At each time $t$, this module updates the memories of nodes within an $\alpha$ hop-distance of the edge event $\mathcal{E}_t$.
Updates are performed using a GRU cell \citep{Chung_Gulcehre_Cho_Bengio_2014}.
Formally, the memory of a node $v$ at time $t$ is
\[m_t(v) = \left\{\begin{array}{cl}
    \texttt{GRU}\big([m_{t-1}(v)~||~s_t(v)], m_{t-1}(v)\big) & \text{if $v$ is within an $\alpha$ hop-distance of $\mathcal{E}_t$}; \\
    m_{t-1}(v) & \text{otherwise}.
\end{array}\right.\]


\paragraph*{Local Aggregation Module.}

This module updates the estimates---i.e., probabilities of membership in the MaxIS solution---of all nodes within a $\beta$ hop-distance of the edge event $\mathcal{E}_t$.
First, each node $v$ aggregates its immediate neighbors' memories:
\[\tilde{h}_t(v) = \texttt{ReLU}\left(\sum_{u \in \mathcal{N}_t(v)}\mathbf{W}_1 m_t(u)\right),\]
where $\mathbf{W}_1$ are learnable weights.
Next, node $v$ combines these aggregated memories with its own memory and degree information to obtain a local embedding
\[h_t(v) = \mathbf{W}_2\left[\tilde{h}_t(v)~||~m_t(v)~||~d_t(v)\right],\]
where again $\mathbf{W}_2$ are learnable weights.
Finally, node $v$ passes its embedding through a step-down MLP and a sigmoid function $\sigma$ to obtain its estimate (probability of MaxIS membership) in $[0, 1]$:
\[p_t(v) = \sigma\big(\texttt{MLP}(h_t(v))\big).\]
Any node $v$ beyond the $\beta$ hop-distance of $\mathcal{E}_t$ simply retains its previous estimate, i.e., $p_t(v) = p_{t-1}(v)$.


\paragraph*{Training.}

We first define a loss function from a node's local perspective and then define a cumulative loss function from these local losses.
Since the memory and local aggregation modules operate on individual nodes, a local loss function enables these modules to synergize and learn a distributed update mechanism.
The loss for a node $v$ at time $t$ is
\[\ell_t(v) = -p_t(v) + \frac{c}{2d_t(v)} \sum_{u \in \mathcal{N}_t(v)} p_t(u)p_t(v).\]
The $-p_t(v)$ term rewards nodes with large estimates (i.e., probabilities of MaxIS membership closer to 1), aligning with the goal of finding a large independent set.
This is counterbalanced by the $\sum_{u \in \mathcal{N}_t(v)} p_t(u)p_t(v)$ term which penalizes violations of independence, i.e., when neighbors $u$ and $v$ both have large estimates.
This constraint violation term is normalized by the degree of $v$ and is then halved to compensate for double-counting the loss from the perspectives of both $u$ and $v$.
Finally, the constant $c$ is a hyperparameter balancing the two terms' loss contributions; in practice, $c = 3$ worked reasonably well across our evaluations (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).

The cumulative loss at time $t$ is the sum of all local losses $\ell_t(v)$ for nodes $v$ within a $\beta$ hop-distance of the edge event $\mathcal{E}_t$ (i.e., all nodes that updated their estimates at time $t$).
This set of nodes at this one time step forms a single batch in our training process.
By setting $\beta$ as an appropriate function of dynamic graph size (see Model Variants, below), batch sizes will automatically scale to any input dynamic graph and provide sufficient inference samples for training.
The sequential processing of all edge events in a dynamic graph's training set forms a single epoch in our training process.
In a single run, we train until the losses for epochs have stabilized and select the epoch with the least loss.


\paragraph{Model Variants.}

As explained above, the hop-distances $\alpha$ and $\beta$ control which nodes utilize an edge event to update their memories and which nodes update their estimates and contribute to cumulative loss, respectively.
In the \underbar{\textit{bounded cascading} (BCAS)} model variant, we set $\alpha = \beta = \gamma \cdot \text{diam}(G_0)$, where $\gamma \ll 1$, strictly bounding nodes' memory and estimate updates to a local region around each edge event.
This variant mimics rule-based approximate update algorithms for MaxIS which analogously spread out from the point of the edge event, updating nodes' memberships to generate a new MaxIS \citep{Chechik_Zhang_2021,behnezhad_fully_2019}.
Locally bounding these update regions typically yields very fast update operations, at the potential risk of missing better MaxIS solutions that require far-reaching updates.

In the \underbar{\textit{non-cascading} (No-CAS)} model variant, we set $\alpha = 0$ and $\beta = \text{diam}(G_0)$; i.e., only the two nodes directly involved in the edge event update their memories, but all nodes in the graph update their estimates and contribute to cumulative loss.
In this variant, the local aggregation module is primarily responsible for the graph-wide learning of the update mechanism, with node memory aiding in the locality of the edge event.
This variant is important for dense graph topologies and edge dynamics where frequent update cascades may disrupt relevant information in nodes' memories.


\paragraph*{Pre-Training and Unsupervised Learning.}

Many rule-based update algorithms for MaxIS crucially require an existing MaxIS solution for the initial snapshot $G_0$ which is later updated as the graph structure changes.
We mitigate this (potentially very expensive) prerequisite by utilizing a pre-training phase that efficiently produces memories for all nodes in $G_0$ and model weights that training can warm-start from.
The idea is to build up $G_0$ one edge at a time (as a sequence of edge addition events), updating only the memories of the nodes involved in each edge addition.
Once $G_0$ is fully constructed, estimates and corresponding losses are computed for all nodes.
This constitutes a single epoch of pre-training; in a single pre-training run, we execute epochs until the cumulative loss has stabilized.
After executing multiple pre-training runs with random seeds, we extract the node memories and model weights from the run with the least cumulative loss to warm-start model training.
Thus, the model as a whole learns to first find a candidate MaxIS (pre-training) and then maintain it (training) in an entirely unsupervised manner.


\paragraph*{Integral Solution Generation.} 

During testing and deployment, we use a simple rounding and violation removal procedure to convert the model's relaxed node estimates into a candidate MaxIS.
Specifically, all nodes with estimates 0.5 or larger are initially included in the candidate solution; then, nodes violating independence are removed in decreasing order of number of violations until an independent set is obtained.
Such a correction/completion procedure is a necessary part of single-shot learning methods that directly use relaxation to solve optimization problems with hard constraints \citep{Schuetz_Brubaker_Katzgraber_2022,Donti_Rolnick_Kolter_2020,Wang_Wu_Yang_Hao_Li_2022,Karalias_Loukas_2020}.



\section{Experiments} \label{sec:experiments}

In this section, we first describe our experimental methodology and then present and interpret the model evaluation results.


\subsection{Experimental Methodology}

\paragraph*{Datasets.}

Ensuring that our experiments are as comprehensive as possible, we include both synthetically created as well as publicly available real-world datasets.
Note that we build our dynamic graphs out of static graphs having similar characteristics. 
Specifically, we utilize their characteristic degree distribution as a basis to generate instances of graphs, which are then chained via edge events (additions or deletions) to form a dynamic graph.
The synthetic graphs include Erd\H{o}s--R\'{e}nyi and Power Law graphs, while the real-world graphs are built using the TWITTER \citep{snapnets}, GERMANY \citep{sndlib}, and BRAIN \citep{Crossley2013} datasets.
We select these real-world graphs due to their diverse topologies: TWITTER is a social network with dense small-world topology, BRAIN is a biological network (characteristic of sparse small-world topology), and GERMANY is a geological network with a mesh topology.
%For the synthetic graphs, we vary not only the initial topology (various graph sizes and average node degrees) of the starting snapshot but also the mechanisms used to generate successive snapshots, thereby capturing a wide range of dynamic behaviors.
%For the real-world datasets, we aim to preserve the inherent structural characteristics observed in the initial snapshot.
The dynamic graphs we look at span a range of sizes---small (\numprint{100} nodes, \numprint{50000} time steps), medium (\numprint{1000} nodes, \numprint{100000} time steps), and large (\numprint{10000} nodes, \numprint{5000} time steps). Each dynamic graph is divided into its training, evaluation, and testing splits, arranged chronologically with increasing time steps. We use a 70:15:15 training, evaluation, testing split for small datasets and a 50:25:25 split for medium datasets. The large datasets only have \numprint{5000} time steps because we use them solely for generalization experiments and do not train our model on them.
Further details on dataset generation and dynamics will be added to the Appendix.


\paragraph*{Our Model and Comparisons.}

We ran our complete pre-training and training pipeline using the two model variants: BCAS and No-CAS. For our experiments with the BCAS variant, we set $\alpha = \beta = 0.25 \cdot \text{diam}(G_0)$, i.e., half of the radius of the first snapshot of the dynamic graph.

To keep our comparisons balanced and complete, we compare against a variety of existing works for finding Maximum Independent Sets.
While we compare to the work by \citet{Zheng2019}, which is designed for dynamic graphs, all the other works we consider are intended for static graphs.
For these, we compute the solution for each snapshot in the dynamic graph independently.
\begin{itemize}
    \item One of the rule-based update algorithms for maintaining MaxIS in dynamic graphs, referred to as Update-Algo moving forward \citep{Zheng2019}.
    \item Current state-of-the-art GNN-based machine learning model for MaxIS in static graphs: comparator model by \citet{Brusca2023}, referred to as DP-GNN in this work.
    \item As an approximate heuristic search method, we run the deterministic greedy algorithm, referred to as Greedy-MaxIS in the results.
    \item Gurobi, the most commonly used and commercially available mixed integer programming solver \citep{gurobi}. 
\end{itemize}

The reported results for our model variants correspond to the best performance across three runs with different random seeds.
We train and test our model variants and all the other algorithms on a AMD EPYC 7413 CPU with single A100:20gb GPU machine.
For the learning methods, training was halted after seven days; for Gurobi, we enforced a time limit of 1~s.


\paragraph*{Metrics.}

We compare our model against the different methods on the specified datasets using three metrics: approximation ratios, peak memory usage, and per-graph solution generation time.
The approximation ratio (referred to as performance) refers to the factor by which an algorithm's solution deviates from the optimal solution of a problem.
In our case, it is defined as the ratio of the MaxIS solution obtained by a method to the corresponding solution produced by Gurobi, which we use as our baseline.
Peak memory usage denotes the maximum memory consumed by each method while training on a given dataset.
Per-graph solution generation time (referred to as runtime) refers to the time taken by a method to process a single snapshot and generate a solution.
We report the \emph{average} approximation ratio and runtime (in seconds per graph, s/g) over the entire test dataset, which consists of a sequence of snapshots (time steps) for each dynamic graph.



\subsection{Results}

The results draw out the efficient scalability and performance of our model, where both our model variants outperform DP-GNN across all the medium-sized datasets (Table \ref{tab:results_500}), both in terms of performance (\numprint{1.6}--\numprint{5.4}x better) and runtime (\numprint{200}--\numprint{1220}x faster). DP-GNN gets handstrung by its autoregressive approach, and its enormously slow runtime even prevented us from evaluating it on the entire test dataset. Adding on to that, we are much more memory efficient and consume \numprint{11.5}--\numprint{27.6}x less memory in comparison. Even when compared on small-sized datasets (Table \ref{tab:results_250}), our model variants perform fairly well while the BCAS variant is \numprint{1.3}--\numprint{2.6}x faster in runtime.

Our BCAS variant compares favourably to Greedy-MaxIS in terms of performance on the BRAIN dataset, while having almost the same runtime. While only slightly worse in performance on the ER and PL datasets, we are still \numprint{2}--\numprint{4.8}x faster in runtime. We draw out this observation to argue that our method can perform better than a Greedy-MaxIS method, and Greedy-MaxIS is not necessarily a better option as posited by \citet{Angelini_Ricci-Tersenghi_2023}. 

On the medium-sized datasets, we almost always outperform Gurobi in at least one of our variants. At the same time, our runtime is only a fraction of a second compared to Gurobi's 1s time limit.


\paragraph*{Generalization.}

We highlight our model's generalization ability by using our model variants trained on small-sized synthetic datasets to evaluate on the corresponding large-sized datasets (Table \ref{tab:results_generalization}), which are \numprint{100}x larger%a factor $10^2$ bigger 
